\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Wary dollar rebounds against yen. \b0\par\par\uc2 703 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 1999\par 11:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 3 (Reuters) - The dollar staged an overnight comeback and sailed above 115 yen on Tuesday as the yen was hit after remarks by Japanese Foreign Minister Kiichi Miyazawa sparked fears of joint U.S/\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar, which had plunged to a five-and-a-half month low of 113.80 yen on Monday, rose nearly 1.5 percent after Miyazawa said he and U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers had agreed to keep in close contact on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves and that Japan and the U.S. viewed sharp \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations as undesirable. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said Miyazawa's comments had been used as an excuse to buy back dollars and that the rally could soon fade. \par \par \uc2  "I do not think the market really believes the U.S. are interested in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  at these levels," said a London-based U.S. bank trader. "Basically the market was very short of dollars yesterday and the Miyazawa comments just came in handy." \par \par \uc2  By 0752 GMT, the dollar was at 115.40/45 yen from 114.21/31 in late European trading on Monday. Technical analysts said the dollar's advance was facilitated by a favourable chart pattern. \par \par \uc2  "The technical indicators are breaking higher from oversold territory, but we really need to see resistance at 115.80 overcome in order to sustain the recovery and move to the 117.30 and possibly even 117.70 area," said Ian Stannard, technical analyst at Chase Investment Bank. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa's hints at a possible U.S. help on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  also pressured the yen against the euro. Euro/yen rose to 123.03/06 yen, gaining more than a yen from 121.85/95 late in Europe on Monday. \par \par \uc2  However, analysts said they were sceptical at the degree of co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  agreed over the phone between the two top officials and cast doubt on the willingness of the United States to participate in a round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at this stage. \par \par \uc2  "Miyazawa was probably throwing us a red herring by suggesting that the Treasury are on the cusp of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Steve Barrow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bear Stearns. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that although the threat of co-ordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action would keep the yen under pressure for the time being, if the U.S. did not indicate they were interested in joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  the yen could soon restart its uptrend. \par \par \uc2  "Unless we see words translated into action, this is going to be a very temporary move," said Jeremy Hawkins, chief economic adviser at BankAmerica in London. \par \par \uc2  Away from the main spotlight, the euro was quietly idling around the mid-$1.06s area, after failing to break above the key $1.07 hurdle in Asian trading. Euro/dollar was at $1.0660/62 from $1.0668/70 in late Monday trading in Europe. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the market was still puzzled by the lack of reaction in the euro to a batch of encouraging economic surveys released in the euro zone on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Hawkins at Bank of America said this could be explained by the fact that the euro had already staged an impressive recovery since the release of the German IFO survey, the first positive euro-zone survey in recent times. \par \par \uc2  The magnitude of the move, which saw euro/dollar gain six percent in two weeks, suggested the market had priced in better prospects for the euro-zone economy. With no fresh data due out on Tuesday the euro was seen continuing to move sideways. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere in Europe the pound was little changed against the dollar and the euro. Analysts welcomed a report released by UK mortaged lender Halifax showing a sharp rise in house prices which supported expectations of a British interest rate hike and was seen bolstering the pound. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was at $1.6152/57 compared with $1.6144/54 on Monday. Euro/sterling was stable just below 66 pence. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc was also steady, trading around 1.50 per dollar, but had yet to tackle a resistance at 1.5115 which chartists said would negate downside pressure. \par \par \uc2  ((Lisa Jucca, London Capital Markets 0171 542 6745, elisabetta.jucca@reuter.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv83038xi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
