\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar on defensive, bears lose  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  fear. \b0\par\par\uc2 671 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 August 1999\par 02:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 2 (Reuters) - The dollar looked destined for further losses against the yen this week after offshore bears scorned the risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and hammered the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 5-1/2   month lows against the yen late on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said a good U.S. name and operators from the IMM options pit in Chicago led the selling spree, driving the dollar as low as 114.20 and sundering technical support at 114.50. This was a major retracement target and its loss was very bearish with analysts tipping 113.60 as the next goal. \par \par \uc2  It did edge up to 114.65/75 by 2300 GMT in early Tokyo trade on Monday, but only because dealers here retain some anxiety about possible yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Overseas investors, however, have gradually lost their fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  having seen it fail miserably over the past couple of months. \par \par \uc2  Data from the BOJ on Friday suggested the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had bought $12.11 billion in July on top of more than $20 billion in June, and yet over that period the dollar has declined about five percent against the yen. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese authorities have also softened their verbal stance, conceding that constant \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not clever and shifting from a commitment to prevent yen strength to merely acting against too rapid a yen rise. \par \par \uc2  Dealers suspected the shift in part reflected the realisation that the latest slide has as much to do with dollar weakness as yen strength and thus could not be combatted by the Japanese alone. \par \par \uc2  This has seen the authorities here trying to emphasise the threat of a firmer yen to the United States and the rest of the world in the hope of securing concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "They're trying to get them on board and you can sense the U.S. is becoming more uneasy about the weakening trend," said a U.S. bank dealer, noting Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers' reiteration of the strong-dollar policy last week. \par \par \uc2  That stirred speculation the U.S. might eventually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though sellers were betting the dollar would have to fall closer to 110.00 before the Treasury overcame its traditional reluctance to interfere in  markets. \par \par \uc2  That view reinforced the bearish chart outlook in the short term with the next target being 113.55/60 yen, a reaction low from February 11, enroute to the 111.40/60 area which provided support in February and in October last year. \par \par \uc2  Resistance was seen around 115.00 with more at 115.55. \par \par \uc2  The euro softened at the start to stand at $1.0680/90 from $1.0707/14 late in New York on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The euro was still ahead on the week thanks to signs the European economy was picking up momentum, while the growing risk of a U.S. rate hike made assets markets there a little less attractive. \par \par \uc2  However, the euro failed repeatedly at stiff $1.0745 resistance, stirring speculation the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quietly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent an uptrend forming. \par \par \uc2  That had analysts looking for a test of support around $1.0650 in the near term, though most expected $1.0590 to provide a solid base. A break of $1.0745, meanwhile, would open the way to the major retracement target at $1.0800. \par \par \uc2  The euro was also weighed by persistent Japanese selling of euro/yen for hedging purposes, which saw it soften to 122.61/77 yen this morning from 123.62 late here on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, sterling ran into profit-taking after a spectacular run-up last week and eased to $1.6154/64 from a three-month high around $1.6250. \par \par \uc2  The main economic event on Monday will be the release of the U.S. NAPM survey for July with the market braced for strength after the Chicago area Purchasing Management survey released on Friday beat forecasts. \par \par \uc2  ((Wayne Cole, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8584 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv8209819\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
