\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Dollar gets some breathing space. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,213 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 August 1999\par 17:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 2 (Reuters) - The battered dollar won a reprieve across the board in European trading on Monday, although it did fall to new five-month lows against the yen earlier before managing to retrace its losses. \par \par \uc2  The euro's upward momentum appeared to be waning somewhat after reaching 2-1/2 highs above $1.07 last week, while wariness over Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept speculative dollar/yen shorts quick to take profits, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "It's a bit surprising given we had quite a good run of data out of the euro area," Francis Breedon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Lehman Brothers in London, said of the subdued euro. \par \par \uc2  The euro at one point pulled back nearly one cent below last week's peaks, despite robust European purchasing managers' data. It was trading at $1.0652/56 at 1443 GMT against Friday's $1.0744 peak and $1.0686/96 late European level. \par \par \uc2  Overbought technical conditions, a selloff in European asset markets amid the strong economic data, and lingering concern about the European monetary union's stability made the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  prone to profit-taking, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The July euro zone purchasing managers index came in at 53.0, its highest since August 1998, against 51.6 in June, sparking concern about eventual European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tightening. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said worries over EMU stemmed from an International Monetary Fund working paper published on the IMF's website last week suggesting that the euro could break apart if individual member countries faced big external shocks and were unable to agree a common monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  The report was written by economists from Germany's Wuerzburg University including a former visiting scholar at the IMF's research department. \par \par \uc2  "There's nothing really new in it, but it seemed to have been talked about quite a lot today," said Toru Sato, a strategist at Dai-Ichi Kangyo Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen had bounced by more than half a yen from early five-month lows below 114 yen by late European afternoon. A weaker-than-expected U.S. National Association of Purchasing Management index for July helped the dollar extend the rebound. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was last trading at 114.65/75 yen against 114.75/85 late in Europe on Friday after briefly hitting a low of 113.90 in the morning, its lowest since February 15. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said speculative traders have been quick to take profits on their short dollar positions in the past few sessions because of the risk of getting caught off guard by possible BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  While such position-squaring has kept the dollar from dropping sharply in one go, DKB's Sato said it was discouraging the build up of short positions in the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "On the surface, it looks choppy, but it's been a profitable environment for speculators just selling the rally," he observed. "The market seems to have found a rhythm that might not be broken until the Japanese authorities actually come in." \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the BOJ might be merely waiting for the dollar's downtrend to wear itself out but that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could also be planning a coordinated action with the U.S. Federal Reserve at some point. \par \par \uc2  "They are either waiting for the market to play itself out or are going to come in with some strategy that is more powerful than the previous one, which obviously didn't work," Breedon at Lehman said. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, sterling pulled back more than a cent from Friday's two-month highs against the dollar and dollar/Swiss franc was also more than a centime off Friday's lows, taking its cue from the euro's retreat. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was last trading at $1.6145/55 versus Friday's $1.6250 high and $1.6195/6205 late European level. Dollar/Swiss was at 1.4962/72 against 1.4932/42 late in Europe on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The euro at one point pulled back nearly one cent below last week's peaks, despite robust European purchasing managers' data. It was trading at $1.0652/56 at 1443 GMT against Friday's $1.0744 peak and $1.0686/96 late European level. \par \par \uc2  Overbought technical conditions, a selloff in European asset markets amid the strong economic data, and lingering concern about the European monetary union's stability made the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  prone to profit-taking, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The July euro zone purchasing managers index came in at 53.0, its highest since August 1998, against 51.6 in June, sparking concern about eventual European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tightening. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said worries over EMU stemmed from an International Monetary Fund working paper published on the IMF's website last week suggesting that the euro could break apart if individual member countries faced big external shocks and were unable to agree a common monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  The report was written by economists from Germany's Wuerzburg University including a former visiting scholar at the IMF's research department. \par \par \uc2  "There's nothing really new in it, but it seemed to have been talked about quite a lot today," said Toru Sato, a strategist at Dai-Ichi Kangyo Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen had bounced by more than half a yen from early five-month lows below 114 yen by late European afternoon. A weaker-t