\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr falls to 114.31 yen, hits fresh 23-week low. \b0\par\par\uc2 370 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juli 1999\par 19:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, July 30 (Reuters) - The dollar extended its losses against the yen on Friday to hit fresh 5-1/2 month lows after slicing past critical support at 114.50 yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar dropped to 114.30 yen its lowest level since February 15 after having started U.S. trading at 115.12/22. The 114.50/55 yen area marked a 61.8-percent Fibonacci retracement of the entire rally from the year's lows near 108 yen to the highs near 125, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "There were some stop losses triggered near the 114.50 level and there has been some selling in euro/yen but the price action is not terribly erratic right now," Jeffrey Yu, senior trader at Sanwa Bank said. \par \par \uc2  The drop occurred shortly after the dollar ploughed through Thursday's 114.89 trough to a low at 114.62. The dollar has now lost over eight percent in value from the year's high against the yen. \par \par \uc2  The sharp drop has accentuated market fears about the chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially as Japanese officials said overnight that they were keeping a close eye on the markets and felt the yen was too strong after it it moved into the 114 area. But dealers remained unsure of what level may be seen as critical by the bank. \par \par \uc2  The yen's recent rise has been seen as an obstacle to Japan's fragile economic recovery and the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has conducted a series of high profile \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  since June 10 to rein in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Last week the Bank of Japan sold yen for dollars twice, once enlisting the Federal Reserve Bank of New York's help and once selling on its own. But it has not been seen since. \par \par \uc2  General pressure on the dollar intensified on Thursday when stronger than expected employment costs data sparked new talk of a possible interest rate hike and torpedoed U.S. asset markets. More strong U.S. data on Friday and losses on the stock market kept hurt the dollar more. \par \par \uc2  ((-Svea Herbst-Bayliss, NA Treasury Desk, 212-859-1638 )). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7u03joh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
