\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar accelerates losses after piercing 115 yen. \b0\par\par\uc2 288 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juli 1999\par 17:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, July 30 (Reuters) - The dollar extended morning losses and hit a new 23-week low at 114.62 yen in early trading, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar easily sliced through 115 yen and then slipped to 114.62 yen as stop loss orders were triggered, dealers said, adding however there was some support for the dollar near 114.50 yen. \par \par \uc2  "The interest is really on the cross and there is some support at 114.50 yen, so I don't think this thing is going to continue to crash," said John Schein, vice president at Generale U.S.A. Financial Financial Markets. \par \par \uc2  Euro/yen slipped to 122.68 yen from 123.24 yen in earlier trading. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has now lost over two percent in value against the yen since the beginning of the week and dealers therefore became more nervous about the chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially as Japanese officials said overnight that they were keeping a close eye on the markets. \par \par \uc2  The yen's recent strength has been seen as an obstacle to Japan's fragile economic recovery and the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has conducted a series of high profile \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  since June 10 to rein in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Last week the Bank of Japan sold yen for dollars twice, once enlisting the Federal Reserve Bank of New York's help and once selling on its own. But it has not been seen since. \par \par \uc2  General pressure on the dollar intensified on Thursday when stronger than expected employment costs data sparked new talk of a possible interest rate hike and torpedoed U.S. asset markets. \par \par \uc2