\par\par \b \uc2  POLL-European funds buy Japanese bonds on yen bet. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrew Priest \par \uc2 605 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juli 1999\par 10:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 30 (Reuters) - European global fund managers are shifting assets into Japanese bonds, betting on further gains for the resurgent yen. \par \par \uc2  A monthly Reuters poll of continental Europe based global fund managers released on Friday showed average allocations to Japanese bonds rose to 7.9 percent of global fixed income portfolios from 6.4 percent in June's poll. \par \par \uc2  Fund managers cited the prospects of further yen strength and the strongly underweight position most funds held previously as the main drivers of the push to own more Japanese bonds. \par \par \uc2  "We moved up our Japanese weighting hoping to profit from the yen move," said Jean-Francois Tilquin head of fixed income investment at Expertise Asset Management in Paris. \par \par \uc2  "The idea was not to be exposed for too long in terms of duration but to profit from the strength of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said. "Although we don't want to play too much against the Bank of Japan." \par \par \uc2  The yen rose to under 115 yen against an ailing U.S. dollar on Thursday, its strongest level for more than five months. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has benefited from renewed confidence in its economy and from the fact that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has remained on the sidelines, eschewing \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that had previously kept the yen close to a 118-120 trading range. \par \par \uc2  Tilquin said the 115 yen level was likely to be strongly defended by Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , adding there was a strong temptation to take some profits at current levels. \par \par \uc2  Other fund managers agreed with this cautious line. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese bonds are vulnerable to a build up of debt by the government and either an economic upturn or a desperate monetisation of issues as politicians despair of a rebound in the economy," said Guy Monson, chief investment officer at Sarasin Investment Management. \par \par \uc2  The yen could overshoot to up to 105 to the dollar if foreigners continue to increase weightings to Japanese shares and domestic investors stop diversifying overseas deterred by a rising yen, he said. \par \par \uc2  "Then you have a situation where you have all buyers and just one seller, the Bank of Japan, which will be overwhelmed," he said. \par \par \uc2  In the Reuters poll allocations to Japanese equities were shaved back in July to 11.1 percent of average global equity funds from 11.3 percent in June. \par \par \uc2  A weak yen has been a major bonus for recovering Japanese companies, boosting their competitiveness abroad at a time when their domestic markets remain sluggish. \par \par \uc2  As the yen has risen so shares in Japanese exporters have suffered, with a number of large corporates citing 115 yen as a key level. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, fund managers increased their holdings of U.S. assets and bought more euro zone bonds. Respondents returned their portfolio revisions before the stronger-than-expected U.S. second quarter employment data was announced on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "Even if we get just get one more (U.S.) rate rise I think you're still in a lose-lose situation with U.S. shares," said Monson. "Either earnings fall off because the economy slows or the financial markets come down because (Fed Chairman Alan) Greenspan hits them," he said. \par \par \uc2  The poll of 11 fund managers based in continental Europe was conducted between July 26 and July 29. The global fixed income portfolios are benchmarked against the Salomon Brothers global bond index while the equity portfolios are benchmarked against the Morgan Stanley Capital International World Index. \par \par \uc2  ((London Capital Markets +44 171 542 5113 fax +44 171 583 7239 email:andrew.priest@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7u03ikg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
