\par\par \b \uc2  INTERVIEW-China can maintain yuan-U.S.'s Aaron. \b0\par\par\uc2 397 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juli 1999\par 09:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 29 (Reuters) - China will likely be able to maintain the value of its yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , given its large trade surplus and growing hard-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, a senior U.S. official who recently visited China said on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  David Aaron, undersecretary of commerce for international affairs, also told Reuters Television in an interview that Japan cannot generate an economic recovery by manipulating the yen through exchange market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and boost exports. \par \par \uc2  Asked about China's ability to maintain the yuan, Aaron said the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief had said the yuan was supported by Beijing's trade position, and he agreed with that assessment. \par \par \uc2  Citing China's sizable trade surplus and growing reserves, he said: "Normally this does not put downward pressure on a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . My view is that there's no reason they shouldn't be able to sustain it." \par \par \uc2  On the yen, Aaron declined to comment specifically on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels, but indirectly chided \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  over its recent yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which the authorities say are aimed at preventing a premature yen rise from derailing a nascent recovery in the economy. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think Japan is going to solve its economic problems by manipulating its exchange rate," he said. "They can't export their way out of this one." \par \par \uc2  Only 10 percent of Japan's economy is in export sectors that benefit from a weaker yen, he said, adding that Japan will never return to its former economic vibrancy without reforming the "hidebound" other 90 percent by exposing domestic firms to greater competition. \par \par \uc2  Aaron criticised Tokyo over a lack of progress on a bilateral deal, which he is in Tokyo to review, to open the Japanese public works construction market. \par \par \uc2  U.S. firms have only a tiny $50 million slice of the total market, which is worth an annual $250 billion, he said. \par \par \uc2  Although Tokyo officials say it is difficult to meet U.S. demands, Aaron said the problem could be solved easily if the central and local governments and the ruling Liberal Democratic Party would act decisively. \par \par \uc2  "When the chips are down, they really can take action," he said. "And I'm here to tell them the chips are down." ((Deborah Collis, Tokyo newsroom +81-3 3432-8022 email: tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7t03o4o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
