\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Dollar firms a touch as euro rests. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,163 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juli 1999\par 17:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 27 (Reuters) - The dollar regained a bit of ground against the euro on Tuesday, aided by the Bundesbank's future president saying he did not want Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be too strong. \par \par \uc2  The euro, which had been rising back towards Monday's two-month high above $1.07, dropped 2/3 of a cent when German daily Hamburger Abendblatt quoted Bundesbank President designate Ernst Welteke as saying he did not want too strong a euro for exporters' sake. \par \par \uc2  The comments took the market by surprise and sparked a broad euro sell-off as short-term dealers unwound long euro positions. \par \par \uc2  "Such comments make traders nervous, but it is also only natural that the euro should soften a bit after such a strong rally," said Rob Hayard, economic adviser at Bank America. \par \par \uc2  Traders said it was encouraging that the euro had held above $1.06 and did not threaten a pivotal support at $1.0570/80. \par \par \uc2  "What the market wants to see now is a confirmation of the improved tone of the euro-zone economy," said Hayward. \par \par \uc2  At 1452 GMT the dollar was at $1.0630/34 to the euro, a touch weaker than $1.0650/57 in late European trade on Monday. The dollar was trading at 116.38/48 yen, compared with 116.48/58. \par \par \uc2  The European data slate is thin for the rest of this week but a host of purchasing manager surveys from the euro-zone's largest economies - Germany, Italy and France - will be released on Monday. \par \par \uc2  They are expected to determine whether the euro's rally, sparked by a positive survey from Germany's Ifo economic research instute last week, is sustainable, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said Welteke, scheduled to assume the role of Bundesbank President and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  council member when Hans Tietmeyer retires at the end of August, encouraged profit-taking after the euro's sharp rise. \par \par \uc2  "What we're seeing at the moment is just keeping the (euro) recovery healthy," said Peter Stoneham, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Thomson Global Markets." \par \par \uc2  Crucial for the dollar's near-term outlook is how U.S. asset markets perform, analysts said. Recent losses in stocks and bonds have weighed on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid concern that foreigners quitting the U.S. asset market would sell dollars. \par \par \uc2  Stronger U.S. data, like June's 10.6 percent year-on-year jump in existing home sales, have fuelled talk of another Federal Reserve interest rate rise to prevent economic overheating. \par \par \uc2  There was little reaction to news that the U.S. July consumer confidence dropped for the first time this year, to 135.6 from 139 in June. The index was down to 107 from 114.9. \par \par \uc2  Momentum to push the yen higher, meanwhile, slowed as the Nikkei benchmark stock index closed down 0.16 percent, posting its fourth straight drop as foreigners took profits. \par \par \uc2  Signs that Japan's economy may not be recovering as rapidly as very strong January-March growth numbers indicated have put some of the yen buying on hold. \par \par \uc2  Dealers continued to wait for possible Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the BOJ has stayed away for nearly one week. \par \par \uc2  Dollar gains posted in Asian trading were capped by sales of euro/yen, and by dollar/yen sales by Japanese life insurers above 117, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The British pound retreated below $1.59 after a retest of Monday's one-month high at $1.5949 failed. It was down from $1.5900/10 late on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The pound took little notice from either the Confederation of British Industry trends survey or Prime Minister Tony Blair's speech on Europe in which he reiterated Britain would join a successful euro when the conditions were right. \par \par \uc2  "Such comments make traders nervous, but it is also only natural that the euro should soften a bit after such a strong rally," said Rob Hayard, economic adviser at Bank America. \par \par \uc2  Traders said it was encouraging that the euro had held above $1.06 and did not threaten a pivotal support at $1.0570/80. \par \par \uc2  "What the market wants to see now is a confirmation of the improved tone of the euro-zone economy," said Hayward. \par \par \uc2  At 1452 GMT the dollar was at $1.0630/34 to the euro, a touch weaker than $1.0650/57 in late European trade on Monday. The dollar was trading at 116.38/48 yen, compared with 116.48/58. \par \par \uc2  The European data slate is thin for the rest of this week but a host of purchasing manager surveys from the euro-zone's largest economies - Germany, Italy and France - will be released on Monday. \par \par \uc2  They are expected to determine whether the euro's rally, sparked by a positive survey from Germany's Ifo economic research instute last week, is sustainable, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said Welteke, scheduled to assume the role of Bundesbank President and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  council member when Hans Tietmeyer retires at the end of August, encouraged profit-taking after the euro's sharp rise. \par \par \uc2  "What we're seeing at the moment is just keeping the (euro) recovery healthy," said Peter Stoneham, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Thomson Global Markets." \par \par \uc2  Crucial for the dollar's near-term outlook is how U.S. asset markets perform, analysts said. Recent losses in stocks and bonds have weighed on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid concern that foreigners quitting the U.S. asset market would sell dollars. \par \par \uc2  Stronger U.S. data, like June's 10.6 percent year-on-year jump in existing home sales, have fuelled talk of another Federal Reserve interest rate rise to prevent economic overheating. \par \par \uc2  There was little reaction to news that the U.S. July consumer confidence dropped for the first time this year, to 135.6 from 139 in June. The index was down to 107 from 114.9. \par \par \uc2  Momentum to push the yen higher, meanwhile, slowed as the Nikkei benchmark stock index closed down 0.16 percent, posting its fourth straight drop as foreigners took profits. \par \par \uc2  Signs that Japan's economy may not be recovering as rapidly as very strong January-March growth numbers indicated have put some of the yen buying on hold. \par \par \uc2  Dealers continued to wait for possible Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the BOJ has stayed away for nearly one week. \par \par \uc2  Dollar gains posted in Asian trading were capped by sales of euro/yen, and by dollar/yen sales by Japanese life insurers above 117, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The British pound retreated below $1.59 after a retest of Monday's one-month high at $1.5949 failed. It was down from $1.5900/10 late on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The pound took little notice from either the Confederation of British Industry trends survey or Prime Minister Tony Blair's speech on Europe in which he reiterated Britain would join a successful euro when the conditions were right. \par \par \uc2  ((Astrid Zweynert London Capital Markets + 44171 542 6320)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7r03shq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
