\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Euro/dollar rally takes breather. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,222 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juli 1999\par 14:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 27 (Reuters) - The euro took a breather on Tuesday after its early attempt to extend a week-long rally against the dollar was sidetracked by a report the Bundesbank's president designate did not want it to be too strong. \par \par \uc2  The euro, which had been edging back up toward Monday's two-month highs above $1.07, dropped nearly a full cent on the German newspaper report quoting the Bundesbank's Ernst Welteke and then settled into relatively narrow ranges. \par \par \uc2  "It's fairly typical after the sort of move we saw yesterday," said Ian Gunner, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO in London. \par \par \uc2  "We saw a test of the lows on this Welteke comment...then it's bounced back and it's off again - it's just consolidating." \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.0622/27 at 1050 GMT after peaking earlier at $1.0700 - just short of Monday's $1.0722 two-month high which had capped a gain of nearly six percent in a week. \par \par \uc2  Traders said a lot of shorter-term long euro positions were unwound on the daily Hamburger Abendblatt article quoting Welteke as saying he did not want too strong a euro because it would weaken the euro-zone's export economy. \par \par \uc2  "I think what we're seeing at the moment is just keeping the recovery healthy," said Peter Stoneham, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Thomson Global Markets. "We're just clearing the market that may have gotten a little long." \par \par \uc2  "A lot of people are looking at $1.0840 (May 10 high), and I think it's still a viable target," he noted, adding that he would be concerned if the market pulled back below $1.0470. \par \par \uc2  Stoneham said European economic data, including Britain's, have been looking brighter in recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  "The UK CBI survey suggests that Europe as a whole is beginning to look a little better," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Confederation of British Industry's quarterly trend survey showed that the July manufacturing order balance, at minus 19 versus minus 23 in April, was at its best level since July 1998. The July manufacturers confidence balance, at plus 5 versus minus 6 in April, was the first rise in nearly two years. \par \par \uc2  Traders also noted that the Welteke story was partly offset by remark from his predecessor, Hans Tietmeyer, who said late on Monday that the euro's recovery had so far been "not negative". \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the dollar remained broadly under pressure, with sterling retesting Monday's $1.5946 one-month high and dollar/yen drifting about a yen above Monday's five-month lows. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was last at $1.5905/10 after peaking at $1.5945 earlier - within a whisker of Monday's top. Traders said the positive CBI numbers had largely been discounted and gave only a brief lift to the already plump pound. \par \par \uc2  Sterling also showed little reaction to UK Prime Minister Tony Blair's speech reiterating his government's policy on the euro. Blair said Britain must not close the door but economic conditions must be met before entering the monetary union. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen was hapless in the mid-116-yen area in Europe after an overnight attempt to recover bumped into stiff resistance above 117 yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was last trading at 116.59/64 yen against a high of 117.36 in Asian trading and Monday's 115.42 low. \par \par \uc2  Traders said concerns over possible Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's testimony due on Wednesday would keep the market on its toes. \par \par \uc2  They said the market would most likely test the lows again in the absence of BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But Toru Sato, a strategist at Dai-Ichi Kangyo Bank in London, warned that a further acceleration of the dollar's decline could prompt coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action instead of the BOJ's solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ((Shin Kishima, London Capital Markets +44-171-542-6721, shinichi.kishima@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  "We saw a test of the lows on this Welteke comment...then it's bounced back and it's off again - it's just consolidating." \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.0622/27 at 1050 GMT after peaking earlier at $1.0700 - just short of Monday's $1.0722 two-month high which had capped a gain of nearly six percent in a week. \par \par \uc2  Traders said a lot of shorter-term long euro positions were unwound on the daily Hamburger Abendblatt article quoting Welteke as saying he did not want too strong a euro because it would weaken the euro-zone's export economy. \par \par \uc2  "I think what we're seeing at the moment is just keeping the recovery healthy," said Peter Stoneham, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Thomson Global Markets. "We're just clearing the market that may have gotten a little long." \par \par \uc2  "A lot of people are looking at $1.0840 (May 10 high), and I think it's still a viable target," he noted, adding that he would be concerned if the market pulled back below $1.0470. \par \par \uc2  Stoneham said European economic data, including Britain's, have been looking brighter in recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  "The UK CBI survey suggests that Europe as a whole is beginning to look a little better," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Confederation of British Industry's quarterly trend survey showed that the July manufacturing order balance, at minus 19 versus minus 23 in April, was at its best level since July 1998. The July manufacturers confidence balance, at plus 5 versus minus 6 in April, was the first rise in nearly two years. \par \par \uc2  Traders also noted that the Welteke story was partly offset by remark from his predecessor, Hans Tietmeyer, who said late on Monday that the euro's recovery had so far been "not negative". \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the dollar remained broadly under pressure, with sterling retesting Monday's $1.5946 one-month high and dollar/yen drifting about a yen above Monday's five-month lows. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was last at $1.5905/10 after peaking at $1.5945 earlier - within a whisker of Monday's top. Traders said the positive CBI numbers had largely been discounted and gave only a brief lift to the already plump pound. \par \par \uc2  Sterling also showed little reaction to UK Prime Minister Tony Blair's speech reiterating his government's policy on the euro. Blair said Britain must not close the door but economic conditions must be met before entering the monetary union. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen was hapless in the mid-116-yen area in Europe after an overnight attempt to recover bumped into stiff resistance above 117 yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was last trading at 116.59/64 yen against a high of 117.36 in Asian trading and Monday's 115.42 low. \par \par \uc2  Traders said concerns over possible Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's testimony due on Wednesday would keep the market on its toes. \par \par \uc2  They said the market would most likely test the lows again in the absence of BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But Toru Sato, a strategist at Dai-Ichi Kangyo Bank in London, warned that a further acceleration of the dollar's decline could prompt coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action instead of the BOJ's solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ((Shin Kishima, London Capital Markets +44-171-542-6721, sh