\par\par \b \uc2  Nikkei ends lower on strong yen,worry over economy. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Fiona Graham \par \uc2 923 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juli 1999\par 08:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 27 (Reuters) - Tokyo's benchmark Nikkei average fell back by the close on Tuesday, with export-oriented firms losing ground after the yen resumed its climb against the dollar, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "It's hard for the Nikkei to make headway when there are concerns that a higher yen could damage Japan's exporting companies and affect the fragile economy," said Tetsuya Ishijima, chief strategist at Okasan Securities Co Ltd. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei 225 average fell 28.62 points or 0.16 percent to end the day at 17,462.72. September Nikkei futures were 10 higher at 17,470. \par \par \uc2  The yen was trading around 116.45/50 yen to the dollar by late afternoon, below the five-month high it reached overnight, but up from its 117 yen level early on Tuesday morning. \par \par \uc2  Traders said many Japanese firms were calculating their earnings based on a rate of 115 yen to the dollar, and any rise beyond that could force them to revise earnings projections downward. \par \par \uc2  "115 yen is the brink for exporters," said Ishijima. \par \par \uc2  Traders said that if the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , international blue chips would be likely to rebound, but investors see it as a big risk to buy these stocks with no guarantee of a weaker yen relieving the pressure. \par \par \uc2  "Investors are waiting on further economic news. It's not an environment where you can get really excited about buying stocks," said Kazunori Jinnai, deputy general manager of equities at Daiwa Securities SB Capital Markets. \par \par \uc2  Traders said investors are waiting for a string of news including Japan's industrial production data for June and forecasts for July and August due out on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  They are also waiting for any indication from Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may move towards an interest rate rise. ((Tokyo Equities Desk +81-3 3432 9404 tokyo.equities.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  The yen was trading around 116.45/50 yen to the dollar by late afternoon, below the five-month high it reached overnight, but up from its 117 yen level early on Tuesday morning. \par \par \uc2  Traders said many Japanese firms were calculating their earnings based on a rate of 115 yen to the dollar, and any rise beyond that could force them to revise earnings projections downward. \par \par \uc2  "115 yen is the brink for exporters," said Ishijima. \par \par \uc2  Traders said that if the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , international blue chips would be likely to rebound, but investors see it as a big risk to buy these stocks with no guarantee of a weaker yen relieving the pressure. \par \par \uc2  "Investors are waiting on further economic news. It's not an environment where you can get really excited about buying stocks," said Kazunori Jinnai, deputy general manager of equities at Daiwa Securities SB Capital Markets. \par \par \uc2  Traders said investors are waiting for a string of news including Japan's industrial production data for June and forecasts for July and August due out on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  They are also waiting for any indication from Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may move towards an interest rate rise. \par \par \uc2  But traders said that despite the Nikkei's weakness there is firm support around the 17,250 level, or half of the retracement of a rally between 15,886 on May 28 and 18,623 on July 16. \par \par \uc2  A total of 462.53 million shares changed hands on the first section of the Tokyo Stock Exchange, against 395.35 million on Monday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Broader indexes were mixed. The TOPIX index\tab    of all first-section shares fell 2.21 points or 0.15 percent to 1,434.11. The Nikkei 300\tab    was down 0.31 point or 0.11 percent at 281.62. The second section index\tab    lost 35.01 points or 1.59 percent to 2,172.76.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Decliners outnumbered advancers 698 to 483, with 130 issues unchanged.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Investors fled from international blue chips and high-tech shares into domestic demand issues. Pulp and paper\tab    and food stocks were higher.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Oji Paper  gained 17 yen or 2.64 percent to 661, and Mitsubishi Paper Mills Ltd  rose 5 yen or 2.46 percent to 208.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Chemicals gained, including Nippon Synthetic Chemical Industry Co Ltd up 7 yen or 3 percent at 240, and Ube Industries  up 5 yen or 2.02 percent at 252.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But securities companies\tab    fell in tandem with the overall index, losing 3.25 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Nikko Securities Co Ltd  lost 44 yen or 5.01 percent to 835, and Nomura Securities Co Ltd  fell 31 yen or 2.03 percent to 1,499.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Nomura Securities said on Tuesday it would reduce brokerage commissions by up to 30 percent for transactions via the Internet and up to 20 percent for holders of general accounts in October.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Toyota Motor Co  added 110 yen or 2.91 percent to 3,890 after Nikko Salomon Smith Barney on Tuesday raised its rating for the company to "1M" from its previous "3M".\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Softbank Technology Corp\tab     was up by its daily limit of 3,000 yen, or 6.98 percent, at 46,000 yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The shares have been rising since Friday, when they debuted at 40,000 yen, more than eight times their issue price, amid investor hopes for the potential of Internet-related issues.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((Fiona Graham, Tokyo Equities Desk +81-3 3432 6179 tokyo.equities.newsroom@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7r03qjp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
