\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Declines As Global Growth Shows Improvement \b0\par\par\uc2  By Stephanie Hoo \par \uc2 755 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juli 1999\par 19:55\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar is having yet another down day Monday, as global asset managers grow increasingly bullish on non-U.S. investments. \par \par \uc2  "There's been a change in sentiment toward other assets," David Durrant, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank Julius Baer in New York, said early Monday, as the dollar sank to fresh five-month lows against the yen and two-month lows against the euro. \par \par \uc2  "We're seeing an ebbing of dollar strength," he said. \par \par \uc2  It hasn't mattered yet that Japan's Nikkei stock index fell the last four sessions. It hasn't mattered that European stock indexes are still underperforming those in the U.S. Indeed, Frankfurt's Xetra DAX fell 1.96% Monday as the euro steamed two cents higher. \par \par \uc2  "A lot of this has to do with sentiment," said Kevin Logan, senior market economist at Dresdner Kleinwort Benson in New York. "It's not necessary for European stocks to go up, as long as U.S. stocks are going down." \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones industrials were recently down just 22.47 points, but investors are nervous about high U.S. stock valuations at a time when the Federal Reserve seen in rate-tightening mode. \par \par \uc2  The euro rallied three U.S. cents early last week on massive short-covering - roughly from $1.02 to $1.05 - and Logan said asset managers started their shift late in the week. At 1:45 p.m. EDT (1735 GMT), the euro was trading at $1.0650, down from an intraday high of $1.0724, but still up significantly from $1.0503 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  "People are now beginning to believe the growth story," Logan said. In Germany, for example, factory orders are up, consumer sentiment is rising, and inflation seems to have bottomed out, he said. \par \par \uc2  On the last point, data early Monday showed German consumer prices rose 0.5% in July from June, for a 0.6% year-on-year rise, which adds weight to the view that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't likely to cut interest rates again this year and may even raise them next year. \par \par \uc2  Over in Japan, government spending has forced improvements in the banking system, companies are reporting rising demand, and Nikkei stocks are still far below where they were before the Asia meltdown, Logan said. \par \par \uc2  Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor said last week that Japan saw a net capital inflow of Y5 trillion from January to June, as investors chased the Nikkei's 30% rally. Just last week, the dollar fell about five yen - from a highs above Y121.00 to lows near Y116.00 - even in the face of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  At 1:45 p.m. EDT the dollar has recovered the day's losses to trade at Y116.60, up from an intraday low of Y115.47 and essentially flat from Y116.57 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  Paradoxically, strong growth in the U.S. is also hurting the dollar, as Wall Street gets ever more nervous about the possibility of further interest-rate hikes. \par \par \uc2  Higher rates fundamentally support the dollar, but not if U.S. assets are in a tailspin, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  But some analysts wonder if the euro can continue to strengthen if European stocks don't perform. \par \par \uc2  "If you look at market prices, the moving average on the Nikkei is still pointing up," but, "the FTSE and the DAX really have come off a fair bit and they're still not showing a bounce," said Ram Bhagavatula, chief economist at NatWest Global Financial Markets in New York. \par \par \uc2  He said he thinks the euro's rise has less to do with investment flows and more to do with a re-thinking of what had been overly bearish sentiment on the fledgling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Not everyone thinks Europe is the place to invest," Bhagavatula said. "I don't think the euro is going straight up." \par \par \uc2  And in the U.S., Standard and Poor's stock futures may be softer, but they've shown "no great collapse," he said. \par \par \uc2  All in all, Bhagavatula thinks the euro's gains may well have peaked at $1.0700 and change, while dollar/yen's range has merely fallen to a bottom of about Y115.00 from Y117.00 previously. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dollar bears, for their part, are targeting a euro rise to $1.0925 in\par the medium term and a dollar fall to Y113.00.\par    -By Stephanie Hoo; 201-938-4384\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 26-07-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1755GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  4219 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv7q05n6m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
