\par\par \b \uc2  Funds see 115 yen level key for Japan stocks. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrew Priest \par \uc2 588 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juli 1999\par 14:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 26 (Reuters) - Japanese shares are still attractive for foreign investors despite this year's sharp price appreciation, although further rises in the yen could send equity prices into reverse, fund managers said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Japanese shares have fallen six percent in the last week following the yen's strong upward move against the dollar. The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  broke through the 116 yen floor to the dollar earlier on Monday, its highest levels for more than five months. \par \par \uc2  A weak yen has been a major bonus for recovering Japanese companies, boosting their competitiveness abroad at a time when their domestic markets remain sluggish. \par \par \uc2  "Fundamentally we see no major change to the Japanese market although it is overbought and due for a correction," said Stephen Mitchell, fund manager of the $1.0 billion Fleming Japan Investment Trust. \par \par \uc2  "The most worrying thing is the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Anything stronger than 115 and the economic recovery will become more difficult. The authorities will do everything in their power to keep the yen around 120," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has benefited from renewed confidence in the economy and from the fact that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has remained on the sidelines, eschewing \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that had previously kept the yen close to a 118-120 trading range. \par \par \uc2  Mitchell said the Nikkei index could fall below the 17,000 point level if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  allowed the yen to fall below 115 yen. \par \par \uc2  Matsushita Electric, which maintains a high level of overseas production, said on Friday its business would be damaged if the dollar fell below 115 yen. \par \par \uc2  Japanese shares have risen 26 percent this year, closing on Monday at 17,491, buoyed by the government's more expansive monetary stance and a greater stress on corporate restructuring. \par \par \uc2  "We still believe that Japan is at the start of a multi-year bull market," Mitchell said. \par \par \uc2  Denis Clough, who manages the Schroder Japan Growth Fund, said Japanese stock prices were vulnerable to a stronger yen but were well insulated from concern about higher U.S. interest rates which was upsetting asset prices elsewhere. \par \par \uc2  "This has all been about Japan's problems and it is therefore in Japan's hands to solve them," said Clough, adding that he expected the Japanese market to appreciate around nine percent in the next 12 months. \par \par \uc2  Clough said his optimism stemmed from the fact that Japan was addressing its main problem, the unprofitable corporate sector, which had caused a negative cycle of spiralling debts, weaker tax revenues and lower consumer confidence. \par \par \uc2  "We are not expecting a sensational recovery over the next 12 months because the process is being driven by corporate restructuring and this means the benefits will take time to show through, as happened in the U.S. in 1991 and 1992," he said. \par \par \uc2  Michael Thomas, head of the Japan team at Martin Currie Investment Management, agreed the investment case for Japan rested largely on a belief that corporate restructuring was likely to continue. \par \par \uc2  "We are finding an increasing number of companies that, thanks to restructuring we want to buy," he said in a recent research note. Example included sanitary ware manufacturer Inax Corp., cosmetics firm Shiseido, Asahi Chemical and Sanken Electrical. \par \par \uc2  Thomas said Japan had benefited from $40 billion worth of foreign funds flowing back into its stocks in the last nine months, but another $25 billion would be needed before funds were back in line with their global asset allocation benchmarks. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7q03svs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
