\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Dollar ducks down as euro elevated. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,156 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juli 1999\par 17:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 26 (Reuters) - The dollar shed over one percent against the euro in Europe on Monday, falling to two-month lows amid growing expectations of an economic pick-up in Germany. \par \par \uc2  It also fell to five-month lows on the yen as the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  refrained from stemming the yen's rise by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Strength in the euro and the yen hit the dollar particularly hard as declines in U.S. stocks and bonds were fanning concern that foreigners quitting U.S. asset markets would sell dollars. \par \par \uc2  The euro rose above $1.07 for the first time since mid-May, after risking a brush with parity just under two weeks ago when it fell to a low of $1.0104. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar remains on the defensive, both against a resurgent euro and...the yen which has now been left to its own devices by the Japanese authorities," said Paul Meggyesi, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  The euro rose to a peak of $1.0722, its highest since May 14. It eased back to $1.0652/56 by 1504 GMT, compared with $1.0502/07 late in Europe on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "Parity worries for euro/dollar are now over and we are starting to get into new territory based on concerns about generalised dollar weakness," said David Bloom, strategist at HSBC Markets in London. \par \par \uc2  The euro's outlook started to turn for the better last Tuesday after a stronger-than-expected business climate survey published by Germany's Ifo research institute gave solid foundations to a rally that had begun the night before. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said a target eyed to confirm a full euro turnaround is $1.08, with tough hurdles ahead of that at $1.07 and $1.0740. \par \par \uc2  The $1.08 level has been a focus of market attention since Bundesbank President designate Ernst Welteke said in June the Ecu's (European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  unit) early 1998 rate of $1.08 was the appropriate reference rate for the euro rather than its January launch rate. \par \par \uc2  Monday's rally was underpinned by a rise of 0.6 percent in German inflation in July, up from 0.4 percent in June. Analysts say inflation, virtually non-existent in Europe's largest economy only a few months ago, may have reached a bottom. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the dollar suffered from losses in U.S. stocks and bonds, which outweighed any positive benefits to be gleaned from the possibility of higher U.S. interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones Industrial Average fell around 400 points last week and the U.S. 30-year T-bond yield is now back above 6.0 percent from around 5.9 percent a week ago on growing prospects of higher U.S. interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "In the short-term at least the positive interest rate differential implications of a (U.S.) rate hike are likely to be offset by the potentially negative impact on U.S. asset markets," said Meggyesi at Deutsche. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the dollar's weakness was helping to fuel a rise in the yen, but moves there were based more on flows than on fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  Dealers shrugged of verbal rhetoric by Japanese officials, which included warnings that a rapid, premature yen rise was not appropriate and that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  would take action if needed. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was last at 116.25/35 yen after falling to its lowest since February 15 at 115.47 and versus 116.44/54 late in Europe on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei has fallen over 1,000 points in four days and closed at 17,491 on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Dollar weakness helped lift the Swiss franc to near-ten-week highs at 1.4977 per dollar, while sterling hit a one-month high at $1.5946. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar remains on the defensive, both against a resurgent euro and...the yen which has now been left to its own devices by the Japanese authorities," said Paul Meggyesi, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  The euro rose to a peak of $1.0722, its highest since May 14. It eased back to $1.0652/56 by 1504 GMT, compared with $1.0502/07 late in Europe on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "Parity worries for euro/dollar are now over and we are starting to get into new territory based on concerns about generalised dollar weakness," said David Bloom, strategist at HSBC Markets in London. \par \par \uc2  The euro's outlook started to turn for the better last Tuesday after a stronger-than-expected business climate survey published by Germany's Ifo research institute gave solid foundations to a rally that had begun the night before. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said a target eyed to confirm a full euro turnaround is $1.08, with tough hurdles ahead of that at $1.07 and $1.0740. \par \par \uc2  The $1.08 level has been a focus of market attention since Bundesbank President designate Ernst Welteke said in June the Ecu's (European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  unit) early 1998 rate of $1.08 was the appropriate reference rate for the euro rather than its January launch rate. \par \par \uc2  Monday's rally was underpinned by a rise of 0.6 percent in German inflation in July, up from 0.4 percent in June. Analysts say inflation, virtually non-existent in Europe's largest economy only a few months ago, may have reached a bottom. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the dollar suffered from losses in U.S. stocks and bonds, which outweighed any positive benefits to be gleaned from the possibility of higher U.S. interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones Industrial Average fell around 400 points last week and the U.S. 30-year T-bond yield is now back above 6.0 percent from around 5.9 percent a week ago on growing prospects of higher U.S. interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "In the short-term at least the positive interest rate differential implications of a (U.S.) rate hike are likely to be offset by the potentially negative impact on U.S. asset markets," said Meggyesi at Deutsche. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the dollar's weakness was helping to fuel a rise in the yen, but moves there were based more on flows than on fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  Dealers shrugged of verbal rhetoric by Japanese officials, which included warnings that a rapid, premature yen rise was not appropriate and that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  would take action if needed. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was last at 116.25/35 yen after falling to its lowest since February 15 at 115.47 and versus 116.44/54 late in Europe on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei has fallen over 1,000 points in four days and closed at 17,491 on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Dollar weakness helped lift the Swiss franc to near-ten-week highs at 1.4977 per dollar, while sterling hit a one-month high at $1.5946. \par \par \uc2  ((Astrid Zweynert London Capital Markets, +44 171 542 7792, astrid.zweynert@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7q03pl1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
