\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Dollar/yen holds precarious perch. \b0\par\par\uc2 612 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juli 1999\par 18:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 23 (Reuters) - The dollar managed to keep a foothold above 116 yen on Friday, its five-month low hit in the previous session, but further yen gains could be on the cards in the absence of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  For the most part the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were consolidating after a volatile week, analysts said. Euro/dollar pared its losses slightly after gaining over four percent since Monday. \par \par \uc2  "It is quite surprising how far dollar/yen has got," said Fabio Fraschetti, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Banca Nazionale del Lavoro in London. "It takes quite a lot of courage to sell it knowing you are in dangerous territory for the Bank of Japan." \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen was at 116.37/47 by 1450 GMT, compared with 117.29/34 late in Europe on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Fraschetti said that left to its own devices the dollar could fall as low as 114.50 yen over the next few days. "It is not very clear what BOJ wants to do and in the meantime investors are continuing to adjust their holdings back to yen assests and that is assisting dollar/yen lower," he said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said overnight repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not always the best way to tackle yen strength. Glenn Davies, chief economist at Credit Lyonnais, said it was possible the Bank of Japan would step in again to halt yen strength but the market would push dollar/yen lower to test the bank's resolve. \par \par \uc2  He said the reemergence of concern about the yuan this week as the Chinese economy slackens could also have a bearing on market sentiment. \par \par \uc2  "Pushing the yen weaker tends to add pressure on the Chinese to devalue which is something the powers that run the world economy would probably want to avoid at the moment," said Davies. \par \par \uc2  Euro/dollar traded quietly during the European session on Friday consolidating within a 0.70-cent range after this week's strong gains. \par \par \uc2  The euro was last at $1.0478/83 compared with a two-month high of $1.0574 hit on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "There is certainly a groundswell of opinion now that you are seeing more signs of Euroland picking up," said Davies. \par \par \uc2  "There have been a lot of surveys recently suggesting that foreign investors are starting to think that European assets are good value." \par \par \uc2  However, he said the euro's upturn was not yet assured. \par \par \uc2  "Some of this has been dollar weakness," he said. "The dollar has fallen against the yen and the euro hasn't really moved much against the yen or the Swiss franc." \par \par \uc2  Euro/yen was last at 122.14/27 compared with 123.42/47 late in Europe on Thursday, while Euro/Swiss was at 1.6047/52 versus 1.6063/6065 in late European trade yesterday. \par \par \uc2  Davies said a weak euro helped foster European growth as the economy started to recover and that other reasons for the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness also had to be taken into account. "There have been question marks over how well monetary union is working, and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  comments," he said. \par \par \uc2  ECB Chief Economist Otmar Issing was reported in Germany's Handelsblatt on Thursday, as saying euro zone economic growth of 2.0 percent was not unrealistic this year and he warned the ECB had to pay special attention to emerging price pressures. \par \par \uc2  ECB board member Eugenio Domingo Solans said on Friday, he saw signs of Germany's economy recovering sooner than expected, and he saw no risk for stability from inflation in the euro zone or any need for an interest rate hike. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7n03v8s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
