\par\par \b \uc2  Japan 1999 1H Trade Surplus Down 7.9% On Strong Yen \b0\par\par\uc2  By Arran Scott \par \uc2 354 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 1999\par 03:39\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's trade surplus narrowed in the first six months of 1999, marking the first semiannual decline since 1996, as a stronger yen continued to undermine the competitiveness of the country's exports. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Thursday that Japan posted a merchandise trade surplus of Y6.057 trillion in the January-June period, down 7.9% from the same period in 1998. For June alone, the surplus came to Y1.178 trillion, down 2.9% from last year and marking the third straight month of contractions. \par \par \uc2  The sharply higher yen compared with year-ago levels was the main reason for the decline in the surplus, economists said. For the first six months of 1999, the dollar averaged Y118.30, down 9.8% from Y131.11 a year earlier. \par \par \uc2  If anything, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  factor has loomed larger in recent months. In June alone, the the dollar traded at an average Y121.53, down 12% from Y138.77 a year earlier. \par \par \uc2  "It's the same kind of thing that has been in play for a while," said Michael Naldrett, economist at Dresdner Kleinwort Benson (Asia). "It reflects the strong yen appreciation from a year ago." \par \par \uc2  Japanese financial authorities have expressed concerns in recent months that a yen appreciation could snuff out flickering signs of economic growth before private demand is strong enough to kindle a full-blown recovery. \par \par \uc2  In an effort to keep the strong yen from undermining demand for the country's products abroad, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has carried out several rounds of aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to weaken the yen over the past six weeks. \par \par \uc2  However, despite selling more than $20 billion worth of yen, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains resilient. Thursday morning in Tokyo, the dollar was trading around Y118.35. \par \par \uc2  Around this level, the yen will likely continue to suffocate foreign demand for Japan's products, economists said. "The strong yen effect will affect exports until early autumn," Naldrett said. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 22-07-99 \par \par \uc2  0139GMT \par \par \uc2  1135 4201 62556 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv7m05gx6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
