\par\par \b \uc2  Global Bonds Seen Following Tsys Lower On Greenspan \b0\par\par\uc2  By Michael Casey \par \uc2 673 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juli 1999\par 00:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Investors in Asian and European bond markets Friday are likely to respond much the same way as their counterparts in the Treasurys market Thursday to U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's warning over inflationary risks in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  They'll sell bonds, largely because of the strong correlation between U.S. and other Group-of-Seven markets, especially those of Europe, where government securities are seen as a good substitute for Treasurys. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark 30-year Treasury bond fell 26/32 Thursday to yield 5.962% at the close after Greenspan said in Congressional testimony that the Fed would act "promptly" and "forcefully" to quash inflationary pressures. \par \par \uc2  This raised concerns the Fed may raise interest rates again at its next policy meeting in August. European bond markets will have little immediate choice but to take on board these same concerns Friday and follow their U.S. counterparts lower, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "Europe and the U.S. are going to track pretty close together between now and the next Fed meeting," said Arthur Micheletti, chief investment strategist for Bailard Biehl & Kaiser in San Mateo, Calif. \par \par \uc2  However, the medium-to-longer term offers the potential for a divergence, analysts said. A weaker dollar and a rethinking of the balance of inflation risks between the U.S. and Europe may mean that any immediate back-up in European yields offers a buying opportunity. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par      Narrower U.S.-German Spread Offers Opportunity\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yield spread between 10-year Treasurys and German bunds had\par already narrowed from 163 basis points a month ago to 108 basis points\par as of Wednesday. To a large extent, that narrower spread was fueled by\par remarks from European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Wim Duisenberg, who\par recently suggested signs of fresh European growth had inclined the ECB\par toward tightening rates.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Now, Greenspan may have shifted expectations back toward a view that\par the U.S. will raise rates before Europe does.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Mixed French economic data out Thursday, which showed slightly\par stronger manufacturing figures but a unexpected slide in household\par consumption, did little to spark concerns about unsustainable growth.\par Meanwhile, inflation statistics for the euro-zone, which were also\par released Thursday, offered a similarly benign picture - consumer prices\par rose just 0.9% in June on an annualized basis, down from 1.0% in May.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "This idea of the ECB tightening doesn't impress me," said Noralyn\par Marshall, international economist with the Marina Fiorini Ramirez Group\par in New York.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "You have 0.9% inflation and yet look at the real yields there. There\par is no real sense that inflation is going to take off," she said. "I\par don't see anybody looking for those economies to boom. Decent growth\par maybe, but nothing robust that can raise inflation concerns."\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Markets May Work Against Treasurys\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bailard Biehl & Kaiser's Micheletti cited another reason why European\par bonds could outperform their U.S. counterparts over the longer term - a\par weaker dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The euro rose through $1.0500 for the first time in two months this\par week, while the dollar fell to a five-month low against the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "A year ago, the U.S. benefited from safe haven flows," Micheletti\par said. "Now with the dollar slowing, we might see European capital\par staying at home and that might boost their bond markets."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japanese government bonds are a harder call, though. Yields remain\par unattractively low, economists say, and the risk of volatility is high,\par given the market's susceptibility to changes in fiscal and monetary\par policy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On top of that, the potential for more sharp movements in the yen,\par given the propensity for the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par markets, makes JGBs a risky play for global fixed income investors.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I don't like them for the yields and in the short term, I'd be\par worried about the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," Micheletti said.\par -Michael Casey; 201-938-2009\par -michael.j.casey@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) DOW JONES NEWS  07-22-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 06:23 PM\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dj00000020010827dv7m02r7r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
