\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - Dollar spirals to five-month low vs yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Svea Herbst-Bayliss \par \uc2 615 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 1999\par 19:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, July 22 (Reuters) - The dollar sank to a five-month low against the Japanese yen Thursday, extending the week's sharp losses as an appetite among investors for Japanese assets overcame Tokyo's efforts to slow the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  In thin trading, the dollar plummeted to 116.61 yen, more than 1-1/2 yen below its opening level of 118.25 yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has now lost some 3.6 percent in value against the yen this week. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also sank against Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as the euro jumped to its highest level in two months against the dollar, taking the Swiss franc with it. The euro jumped to a high of $1.0574, while the dollar sank below 1.5220 Swiss francs to fresh 5-1/2 week lows. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is suffering on the sense the global economy is coming back," said Bank of America \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Eric Nickerson, explaining the morning's move. "People also felt the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again just before (Federal Reserve Chairman Alan) Greenspan's testimony," he added. \par \par \uc2  The yen's surge against the dollar gained steam when several key technical levels gave way, pushing the dollar below the 117.50 area where the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had launched its series of summer \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in June. \par \par \uc2  Dealers had expected the slide would accelerate. "We may see a quick drop to the 116.50 yen level because there are a lot of stops around these levels," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar is now trading against the yen near levels last seen in mid February when the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rallied three yen following news the Bank of Japan had cut its short-term interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Traders added that investors who feared being left behind this sharp move were now instrumental in sustaining gains in both the yen and in Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan has repeatedly tried to slow the yen's sharp rise by selling yen for euros and dollars either directly or through the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Federal Reserve Bank of New York. Japanese officials have publicly worried a strong yen could choke off their nation's fledgling recovery. \par \par \uc2  Japan reported a surprisingly strong real 1.9 percent growth rate in the January to March quarter which translates into a 7.9 percent annual rate. \par \par \uc2  In contrast, first quarter U.S. growth stood at 4.3 percent. \par \par \uc2  "It is very unusual for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to coincide with a big event like the Humphrey Hawkins testimony or an FOMC meeting, and so the market's nervousness about more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been pushed out a bit," Barclays Capital senior economist Henry Willmore said. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan may also be delaying another strike as many participants feel recent forays have been ineffective and "eroded credibility", Bank of America's Nickerson said. \par \par \uc2  "They may just stand back and wait for the market to work through the options that are being unwound and then move again," he added. \par \par \uc2  Remarks from Greenspan Thursday that the economy may overheat if productivity levels off and demand stays strong, and that the euphoric rise in stock prices may be unsupportable, were seen as signs that U.S. growth may slow. \par \par \uc2  The euro immediately surged after the Greenspan remarks but the yen, which had already raced ahead, traded largely steady. \par \par \uc2  While the hint of another interest rate hike could be seen to underpin the dollar, dealers said subsequent losses in the bond market actually weighed on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  ((-N.A. Treasury Desk, 212-859-1639 )). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7m0417c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
