\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Yen rallies as BOJ stays away. \b0\par\par\uc2 661 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 1999\par 17:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 22 (Reuters) - The yen rose to its strongest in four months against the dollar in Europe on Thursday, as dealers tested the Bank of Japan's resolve to cap yen strength. \par \par \uc2  The yen strengthened beyond the area of 117.50 to the dollar which has capped its gains since March, and which on June 10 provided the trigger for the first in at least seven yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan stayed out of the market on Thursday, and analysts questioned the success of its repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Whether it helps in the long term remains to be seen," said Michael Rottmann, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HypoVereinsbank Munich. \par \par \uc2  "In the short term they can manage to keep dollar/yen around 118 and euro/yen around 123," he added. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at 117.36/41 yen at 1431 GMT, compared with a trough of 117.27, its lowest since March 24, and one yen below 118.42/47 traded in late Europe on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The euro also weakened against the yen, dropping to a low of 123.11 but holding well above Monday's life low of 120.91. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the BOJ's approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have changed with the appointment on July 8 of top financial diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda in place of the more high-profile "Mr Yen", Eisuke Sakakibara. \par \par \uc2  "It seemed that 120 yen was the line in the sand, but maybe they have shifted," said Joanne Collins, senior economist at Daiwa. "But a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would hurt the Japanese recovery, which is very tentative." \par \par \uc2  Financial markets were watching U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's semi-annual Humphrey-Hawkins testimony, starting at 1500 GMT, for clues on U.S. monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  The Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) raised the federal funds rate by 25 basis points to 5.0 percent at the end of June, but removed its tightening bias. \par \par \uc2  "If the testimony is not too hawkish, the U.S. equity market will have a good time and euro/dollar will come down to $1.0470," said Rottmann. \par \par \uc2  The euro rose to a high of $1.0545 on Thursday, just shy of Wednesday's five-week high against the dollar, before falling to $1.0496/01 by 1439 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the euro had been boosted by a strong German Ifo business climate survey earlier this week and by comments by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg late last week about a tightening bias "creeping in" to the ECB's thinking. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment towards the euro, which has rallied by more than four cents this week from a low of $1.0120 on Monday, was also helped after European Union finance ministers last week agreed a common line on the euro, removing the outspoken political comment which has damaged the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's credibility. \par \par \uc2  But the euro was not an out-and-out buy, analysts said, while it remained capped by critical resistance at $1.0550/60. \par \par \uc2  More evidence of stronger European growth was also needed, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  French industrial production released on Thursday rose an above-forecast 0.6 percent in May. \par \par \uc2  Italian May retail sales rose by a smaller-than-expected 1.2 percent year-on-year. \par \par \uc2  The pound strengthened against all major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Britain's economic secretary to the Treasury Patricia Hewitt said there were no signs the UK could achieve convergence with the euro zone by the end of the current parliament. \par \par \uc2  The pound rose to a peak of $1.5875, its highest in July, compared with a low of $1.5470 set on July 9, its weakest since August, 1996. \par \par \uc2  Sterling recovered from Wednesday's three-month low of 66.87 pence per euro to 66.34/42 pence by 1510 GMT. \par \par \uc2  ((Carolyn Cohn, London Capital Markets +44 171 542 6320, carolyn.cohn@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7m040zj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
