\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar bears retreat in face of tumbling Nikkei. \b0\par\par\uc2 792 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 1999\par 09:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO July 22 (Reuters) - The dollar firmed against the yen by late afternoon on Thursday, helped by buybacks spurred by a sharp fall in Tokyo stocks, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo share prices ended the day down over 500 points. The fall was negative for the yen since it could slow the amount of capital inflows into Japan if such weakness lasts. \par \par \uc2  News that China's Foreign Ministry had called reporters to a special briefing at 0615 GMT also boosted the dollar. The ministry gave no clue as to what the briefing would be about. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at 118.59/64 yen as of 0625 GMT, compared with 118.07/17 yen in late New York on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they were puzzled by the Chinese briefing, and despite the knee-jerk market reaction, they said they did not expect any significant policy announcement. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the euro stood at $1.0501/04 as of 0633 GMT compared with $1.0523/30 in late New York on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The market was watching to see whether the euro would break above a key chart point of $1.0560. If that happened, some dealers said the euro could be set for a rise towards $1.08. \par \par \uc2  The rise could be slowed, however, by persistent demand among Japanese investors and exporters to sell euros for yen at levels above 124.50 yen, they said. \par \par \uc2  In the near term, the focus will be on the first leg of U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's "Humphrey-Hawkins" congressional testimony, to start at 1500 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market would be watching the reaction of U.S. stock and bond markets to Greenspan's remarks. \par \par \uc2  Coming three weeks after the Fed raised a key short-term interest rate, Greenspan's testimony is not expected by the markets to suggest any additional rate rises in the near term. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the slide in Tokyo stocks saved the dollar from a sharp fall. If stop-loss orders near 117.50 yen had been triggered, the next support would have been at 116.90 yen, they said. \par \par \uc2  "The root cause of recent yen strength has been capital inflows to Japan on the back of the strength of Tokyo stocks," said a city bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  The fall in stocks, which dealers said was due to substantial profit-taking interest among foreign investors, raised doubts about the sustainability of this scenario and caused speculators who had been short on the dollar against the yen to buy back, the dealer said. Dealers said there were also vague rumours that strategists at U.S. think tanks and investment banks have been recently recommending to investors to sell Tokyo stocks. \par \par \uc2  Earlier on Thursday, Japan's top financial diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda reiterated that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to block a premature rise in the yen. \par \par \uc2  Asked about market views that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have not been effective in stemming the yen's rise, Kuroda said: "As we have said before, there is no change at all in our \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy." \par \par \uc2  But dealers said there may be some signs of change, at least in the way \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are conducted. They said the aim of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have shifted from pushing up the dollar to slowing its declines against the yen. \par \par \uc2  "Yesterday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  pushed the dollar up by less than one yen, showing that it was only aimed at slowing the dollar's decline," said a dealer at a city bank. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars from around 118.70 yen late in Tokyo on Wednesday, lifting the dollar to around 119.40 yen. \par \par \uc2  That was in stark contrast to the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  seen on July 5, when the BOJ started to buy dollars from levels below 121.50 yen and continued to buy above 122 yen. \par \par \uc2  "Finance Minister Miyazawa's latest comments seem to confirm the market's view that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  now is not aimed at pushing the dollar/yen rate to any particular level," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa told an Upper House financial panel on Thursday: "It is important to pre-empt rapid (\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) movements. Thus, we have sent our message to the market against rapid movmements." Elsewhere, BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami reiterated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep its ultra-easy monetary policy unchanged until the threat of deflation has gone. \par \par \uc2  He said the economy had stopped declining, but there were no clear signs of private demand recovery. \par \par \uc2  The euro stood at 124.42/55 yen as of 0715 GMT, against 124.24 yen in late New York on Wednesday. ((Masayuki Kitano, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 5473 3748 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7m03zh5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
