\par\par \b \uc2  Market mood turns against dollar, has BOJ cornered. \b0\par\par\uc2 695 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 1999\par 02:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 22 (Reuters) - The tide of market sentiment looks to be turning against the dollar after it suffered heavy losses to the yen and European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight. \par \par \uc2  The retreat on the yen was particularly galling for bulls as it came despite further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) late here Wednesday. Dealers noted the dollar had even finished below the pre-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  level, the first time that had happened since the BOJ began buying six weeks ago. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's cause was made all the more hopeless by signs of a lightening in investor gloom over Europe which saw the euro and Swiss franc bound ahead. \par \par \uc2  The dollar did edge up from its New York low of 118.10 to stand at 118.34/37 by 2300 GMT as the more cautious covered short positions in case the BOJ stepped in. \par \par \uc2  But that still left the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  well adrift of the 119.40 yen level reached Wednesday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought dollars in late Tokyo time. \par \par \uc2  Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Haruhiko Kuroda made a point of confirming that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and pledged further decisive action, making renewed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  almost inevitable. "It's a question of face now," said one U.S. bank trader. "Kuroda has to save his credibility." \par \par \uc2  But dealers doubted such action would achieve more than providing the market better levels to sell into. \par \par \uc2  "The MOF is always saying fundamentals should determine \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said the dealer. "Well, the fundamentals of global capital flows say the yen should be higher." \par \par \uc2  Aside from the natural demand for yen created by Japan's huge trade surplus with the United States, offshore investors were rushing to pick up Japanese assets in anticipation of an economic recovery. This point was conceded by BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami on Wednesday when he noted that foreigners had bought a net five trillion yen worth of Japanese stocks this year. \par \par \uc2  Dealers thus thought the bounce from any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would meet substantial selling in the 119.65/80 yen resistance area, while if the BOJ stood aside the dollar was likely to test key chart support in the 117.60/70 area. \par \par \uc2  The latter marked lows from June 10 when the BOJ first began \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  openly and was also the trough touched on Monday. Beneath there was another important low at 117.42, hit in April, and analysts looked for a break of these levels to confirm a downtrend towards 110.00 yen. \par \par \uc2  And, for once, the dollar was having no luck against the euro, where all the talk was of a reversal in fortunes. \par \par \uc2  The euro was up at $1.0510/20 from $1.0436/40 on Wednesday, for a rise of almost four percent since Monday's $1.0120 low. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the rally was partly technical after six months of decline and partly a more general reappraisal of Europe's growth outlook following upbeat German business data. \par \par \uc2  Analysts suspected some consolidation was due near-term but saw a chance of a break past major resistance at $1.0560, being a top from June and a Fibonacci retracement target, which would herald further substantial gains to $1.0800. \par \par \uc2  The improvement in European sentiment extended to the Swiss franc, which zoomed to a seven-week high on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The dollar sagged as far as 1.5220 francs, having finished last week at 1.5743, and snapped major support at 1.5250 in the process. It had bounced a little from an oversold position to stand at 1.5274/86 on Thursday morning, but analysts expected 1.5360 resistance to contain it for a retreat to 1.5120 in the short term. \par \par \uc2  As far as events go, Japanese trade data for June out on Thursday morning illustrated one reason for yen strength, with the surplus against the U.S. rising 18.2 percent year-on-year. \par \par \uc2  The main event will be Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's Humphrey Hawkins Testimony, which the market will be scrutinising for clues on monetary policy. ((Wayne Cole, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8584 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7m03zel\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
