\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Japan, U.S. bury hatchet for now on  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoshiko Mori \par \uc2 803 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juli 1999\par 09:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 21 (Reuters) - Japan and the United States have buried the hatchet for now on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy after the Fed helped the Bank of Japan by buying dollars for yen in New York on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  But Tokyo bankers say there still is a wide gap between Tokyo and Washington on these policies that lies at the core of the recent dollar/yen volatility. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, Japan confirmed that the Federal Reserve Bank of New York had bought dollars on behalf of the Bank of Japan (BOJ). \par \par \uc2  Tokyo bankers estimated that the BOJ's dollar purchases on Tuesday totalled around $1 billion. \par \par \uc2  Takeshi Hanai, executive manager of the Industrial Bank of Japan's International Treasury Department said: "The Fed's assistance in the BOJ's dollar purchases only indicated a possibility that Japan has hinted or even promised Washington it will adopt a large second extra budget later in the year." \par \par \uc2  But it did not signal any narrowing of the gap on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy between the two nations, bankers said. \par \par \uc2  While Tokyo favours a weaker yen to support a still fragile economic recovery, Washington is warming to the idea of a weaker bias in the dollar, particularly against the yen, to deal with its explosively large deficit, they said. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers said on Tuesday: "As for U.S. dollar policy, it is unchanged." But he dropped the word "strong" from the Treasury's usual mantra that a strong dollar is in the nation's best interests. \par \par \uc2  As one proof of a shift in Washington's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, monetary sources said that U.S. Treasury Undersecretary Timothy Geithner criticised \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent aggressive dollar buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for yen when he met top policy-makers of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a visit last week. \par \par \uc2  Summers also said Tokyo should focus on fundamentals rather than \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels, referring to the BOJ's recent dollar purchases. \par \par \uc2  But Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said on Wednesday his ministry would take action to avoid movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market that would hinder a recovery in Japan's economy. \par \par \uc2  Toshihiko Masaki, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund manager at Asahi Bank Ltd, said U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers "was and still is an academic clearly favouring the idea of sinking the dollar to reduce trade deficits". \par \par \uc2  "There is absolutely no evidence that his new position made him immediately trash this belief engraved in his mind." \par \par \uc2  Summers has written enthusiastically about the virtues of a falling dollar as a means to correct international imbalances. \par \par \uc2  In the two weeks since Summers took over as Treasury Secretary in early July, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has taken a dive of over four percent against the yen from the peak of 122.87 yen. \par \par \uc2  "In my observation, Washington has quietly abandoned the strong dollar policy in the last year, no matter what they say in public to the contrary," Hanai of IBJ said. \par \par \uc2  Faced with a large trade deficit, which annoys organised labour and politically influential industries supporting the Democratic Party, Washington favours more a stable dollar with a weaker bias, instead of a strong dollar, Hanai said. \par \par \uc2  "The United States, however, cannot declare the end to its strong dollar policy because that could prompt a nosedive in the greenback by triggering an exodus of foreign capital from U.S. markets," a senior banker at a major Japanese bank said. \par \par \uc2  Other bankers said a U.S. reluctance in spend its own money to help Japan's attempt to prop up the dollar against the yen may change depending on the course of euro. \par \par \uc2  Yoshinobu Onishi, director and general manager of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Treasury Division at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi, said: "Washington may change its policy stance if the dollar starts to tumble against the euro as well." \par \par \uc2  It has already been proved that the dollar is weak against the yen as it has kept falling despite the massive dollar purchases by the BOJ, bankers said. The BOJ is estimated to have bought around $25.65 billion in U.S. dollars and euros against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  through \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in June. \par \par \uc2  "If the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tumbles against the euro, then they would worry more about how to sustain foreign capital inflows by retaining confidence in the dollar than checking an explosive deficit by a weaker dollar," Onishi of BTM said. \par \par \uc2  The Euro rose to 3-week high above $1.0436 in late Tokyo on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Commerce Secretary William Daley said on Tuesday that the U.S. trade deficit was running at an annual rate of $224.8 billion, compared to last year's record deficit of $164.3 billion. \par \par \uc2  ((-Tokyo Treasury Desk (813) 3432-8676 \par \par \uc2  tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7l045rh\pa