\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-BOJ upgrades economy view, battles yen rise. \b0\par\par\uc2  By George Nishiyama \par \uc2 679 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juli 1999\par 11:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 21 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan (BOJ) put a slightly brighter gloss on its economic assessment on Wednesday but cautioned a recovery was still not in sight and continued its battle against a strengthening yen. \par \par \uc2  "The economy has stopped deteriorating and corporate sentiment has improved somewhat," the BOJ said in its monthly report, making an apparent reference to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's "tankan" survey, released earlier this month, which showed an improvement in business sentiment from three months ago. \par \par \uc2  But the BOJ said it still saw no clear recovery in private demand, and BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami, speaking at an afternoon news conference, cautioned that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not change its ultra-easy monetary policy until deflationary fears abated. \par \par \uc2  The improving corporate sentiment, however, has prompted investors, especially foreigners, to pour money into Japanese shares and other assets, driving the yen higher and threatening to thwart the very recovery the government hopes to nurture. \par \par \uc2  Hayami acknowledged that an increase in overseas investors' buying of Japanese shares have led to the yen's rise, and later in the day, the BOJ was seen to have bought dollars for yen from around 118.70 yen in late trading, marking the latest in a string of yen-rise-curbing \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Tokyo authorities. \par \par \uc2  Top financial diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda confirmed that Tokyo authorities sold yen for dollars and said they were prepared to take such decisive action again. \par \par \uc2  "Today in the Tokyo market we took action," Kuroda, the vice finance minister for international affairs, told reporters. \par \par \uc2  The dollar climbed as high as around 119.40 yen shortly after the BOJ's action, but its gains were limited. The greenback was trading at around 118.95 yen as of 0940 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The move follows overnight \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the New York Federal Reserve, which sold yen for dollars on behalf of the BOJ, which showed at least passive U.S. support for Japan's attempt to stem the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the day, Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said Japan would avoid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves that hindered recovery of the world's second-biggest economy. A higher yen makes Japanese exports more expensive overseas and trims the yen value of Japanese companies' foreign earnings. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa also said the Fed's action demonstrated that Japan-U.S. cooperation on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  - called into question by recent remarks from Washington - remained intact. \par \par \uc2  "We couldn't do this if there were not a cooperative arrangement," he told reporters. \par \par \uc2  While Tokyo openly favours a weaker yen to support its fragile economic recovery, traders see Washington warming to the idea of a weaker bias on the dollar, particularly against the yen, to trim its large trade deficit. \par \par \uc2  Asked about recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the market view that there were differences between \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and U.S. policies on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , BOJ's Hayami said: "It is acceptable to conduct \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent rapid movements and to stabilise the market. The United States and Japan have no objections (to such action)." \par \par \uc2  Speaking to a morning news conference, Economic Planning Agency chief Taichi Sakaiya said that while either too rapid a rise or fall in the yen was undesirable, the yen's level against the dollar was not having a serious impact on the economy. \par \par \uc2  He stressed the government must continue to pump money into the economy to ensure that the nascent recovery is not derailed. \par \par \uc2  Sakaiya, quoted over the weekend as saying a stimulative supplementary budget of some five trillion yen ($42 billion) may be needed, repeated his concern that public works spending will drop off in the January-March quarter. \par \par \uc2  He said Japan has no choice but to rely on deficit spending for the time being to support the economy. \par \par \uc2  Separately the government on Wednesday afternoon enacted an extra budget totalling 542.9 billion yen to fund measures to cushion the impact of Japan's near-record unemployment. \par \par \uc2  ($1=119 yen) ((Tokyo newsroom +81-3 3432-8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7l045kz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
