\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr keeps toe-hold atop 119.00 yen, footing shaky. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,027 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juli 1999\par 07:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 21 (Reuters) - The dollar was holding steady above 119.00 yen at Wednesday's midsession as the threat of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  curbed the market's inclination to sell. \par \par \uc2  The nervousness was illustrated by rumours that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  had told the big Japanese banks to keep dealers at their desks over lunchtime, presumably to handle \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Traders scoffed at the talk, but it was enough to keep bears at bay for the moment and by 0345 GMT the dollar was perched at 119.23/28 against 118.98/03 late in New York. However, it twice failed at 119.65 resistance during the morning, and while under there the risks were to the downside, they said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar enjoyed a brief knee-jerk blip higher on news Standard and Poor's had downgraded China's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating a notch to BBB. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market was worried the move would increase pressure for a devaluation of the yuan, speculation about which has grown recently after China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Dai Xianglong proved reluctant to repeat his "no devaluation" pledge. \par \par \uc2  They detected little actual impact on either the yuan or the Hong Kong dollar, however, and were content to wait and see how European markets reacted to the news when they opened. \par \par \uc2  The dollar bounced 1-yen overnight, to as high as 119.85 yen, after the Federal Reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the BOJ, but it soon receded in the face of heavy selling from Japanese institutions and U.S. banks and hedge funds. \par \par \uc2  A host of officials from Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  turned out on Wednesday morning to reiterate the line that premature yen strength was not favourable for the economy. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa hailed the overnight \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 -U.S. cooperation and suggested other nations would tolerate Japan's policy of restraining the yen because they also had a stake in Japan's recovery. \par \par \uc2  Dealers were highly sceptical of his comments, noting that the Fed had a binding agreement to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the BOJ and had shown no intention of acting on its own account. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, U.S. officials missed no opportunity to urge Japan to concentrate on stimulating domestic demand and not rely on exports alone to build a recovery, which markets read as admonition against weakening the yen. \par \par \uc2  "They (MOF) can talk all they want but nobody believes the U.S. is in favour of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said one trader at a European bank. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's on its own and the market scents the weakness, so it's going to keep on pressing." \par \par \uc2  The dollar enjoyed a brief knee-jerk blip higher on news Standard and Poor's had downgraded China's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating a notch to BBB. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market was worried the move would increase pressure for a devaluation of the yuan, speculation about which has grown recently after China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Dai Xianglong proved reluctant to repeat his "no devaluation" pledge. \par \par \uc2  They detected little actual impact on either the yuan or the Hong Kong dollar, however, and were content to wait and see how European markets reacted to the news when they opened. \par \par \uc2  The dollar bounced 1-yen overnight, to as high as 119.85 yen, after the Federal Reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the BOJ, but it soon receded in the face of heavy selling from Japanese institutions and U.S. banks and hedge funds. \par \par \uc2  A host of officials from Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  turned out on Friday morning to reiterrate the line that premature yen strength was not favourable for the economy. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa hailed the overnight \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 -U.S. cooperation and suggested other nations would tolerate Japan's policy of restraining the yen because they also had a stake in Japan's recovery. \par \par \uc2  Dealers were highly sceptical of his comments, noting that the Fed had a binding agreement to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the BOJ and had shown no intention of acting on its own account. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, U.S. officials missed no opportunity to urge Japan to concentrate on stimulating domestic demand and not rely on exports alone to build a recovery, which markets read as admonition against weakening the yen. \par \par \uc2  "They (MOF) can talk all they want but nobody believes the U.S. is in favour of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said one trader at a European bank. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's on its own and the market scents the weakness, so it's going to keep on pressing." \par \par \uc2  He looked for a test of support at 118.70/75 yen once Europe opened and a break there to confirm the bearish technical outlook for a push to 118.30 and the bears' major target at 117.65 yen. \par \par \uc2  The euro, meanwhile, kept a grip on most of this week's sharp gains to stand at $1.0404/14 compared with $1.0145 at the same time on Monday. \par \par \uc2  It bounded higher on Tuesday after an influential German business survey showed a much greater improvement in sentiment than anyone expected. This in turn prompted speculation that German, and thus EU, growth would beat forecasts this year and encouraged investor interest in European assets. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the bounce had improved a dire technical position but the euro needed to clear major resistance at $1.0480 and $1.0560 to suggest its long downtrend had finally found a floor. \par \par \uc2  The situation was similar for euro/yen, with the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  jumping to 123.95 yen on Wednesday from a record low near 121.00 on Monday. The rise cleared resistance from an eight-week-old falling trendline, which was now offering support around 123.85, and briefly topped last week's high. \par \par \uc2  This left analysts mildly bullish for a test of resistance around 124.80 yen and a rally to 125.75 in the short term, although dealers said Japanese institutions and exporters still seemed keen to sell the euro. ((Wayne Cole, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8584 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7l044sy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
