\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Asian shares suffer Wall St hangover. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Wayne Cole \par \uc2 681 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juli 1999\par 05:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 21 (Reuters) - Asian share markets took a spill early Wednesday in reaction to a sharp jolt from Wall Street, while the dollar was under pressure despite overnight \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones industrial index shed 1.71 percent as investors' love affair with high-technology stocks went through one of its periodical spats. \par \par \uc2  The technology-rich Nasdaq index suffered its fourth-largest one-day fall ever, losing 3.47 percent, and it was tech stocks which led the way lower in Tokyo, where the Nikkei 225 index lost 287 points at one point in the morning. \par \par \uc2  "The Tokyo market's recent rise has been on the back of gains in high-techs, so the whole market is likely to be pulled down as high-techs weaken today," said Kunihiro Hatae, equity head at Tokyo Securities. \par \par \uc2  By 0130 GMT the Nikkei was off 267.06 points or 1.44 percent at 18,265.52. \par \par \uc2  But it was still up almost five percent on the month so far and dealers reported undiminished demand from offshore investors betting on prospects of improved earnings from an ongoing wave of Japanese corporate restructuring. \par \par \uc2  They expected losses to be contained by chart support at 18,245 and the uptrend to resume towards last week's high of 18,623. \par \par \uc2  Wall Street's selloff spread across the region, with the Australian All Ordinaries index losing 30 points or almost one percent to 3,097.3. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean market had added worries over the fate of the stricken Daewoo Group and the Composite Stock Price index had sagged almost two percent, losing 19.71 points to 978.77. \par \par \uc2  The Korean government brokered a peace deal between debt-laden Daewoo and creditors on Monday but the market has not been reassured and shares in the group have been sliding sharply since. \par \par \uc2  On the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets all eyes were on the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  after it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the U.S. Federal Reserve on Tuesday to restrain the rampant yen. The Fed was seen buying from 118.30 yen upwards, pushing the dollar as high as 119.80 before stepping back. \par \par \uc2  A host of officials from the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  backed the action with verbal support on Wednesday morning. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa led the way, saying the Fed's action demonstrated Japan-U.S. cooperation on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Haruhiko Kuroda repeated the mantra that a premature rise in the yen was unfavourable for Japan's recovery. \par \par \uc2  But the words failed to convince a sceptical \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, where dealers suspect Japan is far more isolated than it cares to admit, and the dollar eased back to 119.05/10 yen from an early 119.63 high. \par \par \uc2  "The Fed is a reluctant player in all this," said a dealer at a U.S. bank. "It has to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the BOJ because it has a longstanding agreement to do so. But it has shown absolutely no desire to do so on its own behalf, and that's what counts." \par \par \uc2  Without such assistance the BOJ was fighting a losing battle as the worldwide flow of funds favoured the yen, he said. "People want to buy into the Japanese recovery story and that's creating a natural demand for yen that's hard to counter." \par \par \uc2  He expected the dollar to test support around 118.80 yen in the short term and even revisit the week's 117.68 low unless the BOJ stepped in again. \par \par \uc2  The other feature in the market was a rare rally in the euro, which had jumped to $1.0415/25 by Wednesday morning from $1.0145 at the same time on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Much of the gains came after a key German business survey released Tuesday showed surprising strength, stirring speculation that growth forecasts for Germany, and perhaps the whole EU, would have to be revised upwards. ((Wayne Cole, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8584 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7l044p6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
