\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ May  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  In Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Market Wednesday \b0\par\par\uc2 276 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juli 1999\par 22:23\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--With the dollar unable to build on gains made through the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday, traders are wondering if the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will take further action when Asian markets open Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "There hasn't been that much impact on dollar/yen," said a New York-based trader. "The BOJ may well decide it needs to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again to weaken the yen." \par \par \uc2  As reported, the Federal Reserve Bank of New York bought dollars for yen on behalf of the BOJ. Traders said the Fed bought dollars multiple times, first at around Y118.20, then later to keep the dollar above around Y119.20. \par \par \uc2  But at 4:00 p.m. EDT (1930 GMT), the dollar was trading at Y118.97, down from an intraday high of Y119.80 but still up from Y118.30 at Monday's close. \par \par \uc2  At best, the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  produced little more than a one-yen rise in the dollar. By late afternoon, the dollar was somewhat more than half a yen higher than its pre-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  level. \par \par \uc2  U.S. monetary authorities refused to comment on Tuesday's action , but Japanese officials confirmed they had asked the Fed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in what was the BOJ's sixth large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since June 10. \par \par \uc2  Earlier, Japanese officials said they fear yen strength might choke off the country's nascent recovery. The yen has been strengthening early June, when stronger-than-expected first-quarter Japanese growth data encouraged more flows into Japanese stocks. \par \par \uc2  By Robert Flint; 201-938-4408 \par \par \uc2  roberrt.flint@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 20-07-99 \par \par \uc2  2023GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv7k05dx5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
