\par\par \b \uc2  WRAP: Fed Buys Dollars For Yen On Behalf Of BOJ \b0\par\par\uc2  By Stephanie Hoo \par \uc2 711 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juli 1999\par 17:33\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The U.S. Federal Reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Tuesday morning on behalf of the Bank of Japan, buying about $500 million in dollars for yen to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials confirmed they asked the Fed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in what was the BOJ's sixth large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since June 10 but the first in more than two weeks. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the Fed bought dollars multiple times, first at around Y118.20, then later to keep the dollar above around Y119.20. \par \par \uc2  They said the Fed used both the largely transparent Electronic Brokering Systems - EBS - and also made direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  through a bank counterparty. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials have said they fear yen strength might choke off the country's nascent recovery. But the yen has been strengthening since June 10, when stronger-than-expected first-quarter Japanese growth data encouraged more flows into Japanese stocks. \par \par \uc2  "It's true that we entrusted the New York Fed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said an official at the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's New York office. "We arranged for the Fed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on our behalf," said an official at the Bank of Japan's office in New York. \par \par \uc2  U.S. analysts noted, however, that the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  produced little more than a one-yen rise in the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The market's reaction to the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "isn't overwhelming. We haven't been able to get close to Y120.00," said Bob Sinche, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank in New York. \par \par \uc2  At 11:30 a.m. EDT (1530 GMT), the dollar was trading at Y118.98, down from a high of Y119.80 but still up from Y118.30 at Monday's close. \par \par \uc2  John McCarthy, senior vice president at ING Barings Capital Markets in New York, said that fundamentals - especially the growing U.S. trade deficit with Japan - are in favor of a stronger yen, and for that reason the Fed wouldn't buy dollars for yen on its own account. \par \par \uc2  "My guess is the market is long and will look for opportunities to sell. So I don't see a big rally in the dollar/yen even if they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again," McCarthy said. \par \par \uc2  Data Tuesday showed the U.S. trade gap widened to a record $21.34 billion in May, from $18.59 billion in April and exceeding expectations. \par \par \uc2  In Sinche's view, "We think there is still the risk that with Japanese bond yields up, with investors in Japan suffering serious losses on euro-denominated bonds, both from a price point-of-view and a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  point-of-view, there is the potential for more of those funds to come back to Japan." \par \par \uc2  "That flow will continue to support the yen," he said. \par \par \uc2  Until the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducts "unsterilized" \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , allowing considerably more yen into the banking system and returning to an easier monetary stance, the market's bias will continue to lean toward a stronger yen, Sinche said. \par \par \uc2  In its previous five \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the BOJ spent more than $20 billion to buy dollars for yen and euros for yen. By selling its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the BOJ can beef up its own \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves while adding yen liquidity to the market. \par \par \uc2  But U.S. officials have publicly criticized Japan's management of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate. Over the past two weeks, U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers has reiterated that Japan needs to focus on improving domestic demand-led growth. "Manipulating \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not the approach that produces long-run prosperity," Summers said in an interview with CNBC television on July 7. \par \par \uc2  His comments, and the BOJ's absence from the market since July 5, had led some traders to think Japanese officials had been discouraged from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  during U.S. trading hours. \par \par \uc2  As yen-buyers become bolder in the BOJ's two-week absence, the dollar on Monday fell more than three yen at one point, trading as low as Y117.65 from Y120.95 at Friday's close. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japanese markets were closed Tuesday for a holiday.\par    -By Stephanie Hoo, Michael Casey and Sonoko Setaishi; 201-938-4384;\par stephanie.hoo@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 20-07-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1533GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1074 4164 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv7k05d37\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
