\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Euro shakes loose from parity gravity. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shinichi Kishima \par \uc2 688 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juli 1999\par 13:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 20 (Reuters) - The euro soared to three-week highs against the dollar on Tuesday as strong German economic data added to the growing perception that the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may at last have turned the corner. \par \par \uc2  The euro, which had fallen more than 15 percent since its January debut to a low of $1.0104 last week, could now be pulling free of the lure of parity against the dollar, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "I'd say put your parity party hats back in the cupboard," said David Bloom, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC Markets. "The idea of parity for parity sakes is being wiped out by fundamentals." \par \par \uc2  The euro jumped more than two cents in less than 24 hours to a peak of $1.0394 by European midday, its highest since June 28. \par \par \uc2  The rally began in Monday's North American trading on short-covering ahead of Germany's Ifo business climate survey, and accelerated in Europe as the Ifo's headline west German business climate index came in at 92.9, far exceeding the average forecast of 91.1 and a May reading of 90.4. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the writing had anyway been on the wall for the persistent dollar bulls, noting that yields on European bonds had risen sharply in recent weeks in response to signs of an economic upturn in the euro zone. \par \par \uc2  "The only thing that had failed to follow until yesterday was the euro, but now it's playing a little bit of catch-up," Bloom said. \par \par \uc2  Peter von Maydell, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston in London, said his study of yield differentials between U.S. Treasuries and German bunds in the past decade showed that \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  always lagged bonds. \par \par \uc2  "It shows that people had been bearish even though growth expectations in the bond markets have been swinging sharply in favour of the euro and likewise data proving that Europe is recovering," he said. "Ifo isn't even news in that respect." \par \par \uc2  Calculating from the historical relationship between 10-year U.S. T-note and bund yields, a euro/dollar rate between $1.08-$1.10 would be more consistent with growth differentials that debt markets were suggesting, von Maydell projected. \par \par \uc2  He said other European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Swiss franc and the Swedish crown had similar upside potential against the dollar, while sterling was roughly in line with yield levels. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc rallied to its highest levels in three weeks, with dollar/Swiss tumbling to a low of 1.5439 francs from 1.5762/72 in late on Monday in Europe. Sterling/dollar peaked at $1.5818, just short of Monday's $1.5825 three-week high. \par \par \uc2  The latest rally in the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , however, has yet to convince the sceptics. \par \par \uc2  The gains were still within a normal correction to a strong prevailing downtrend, said Yuji Tashiro, a dealer at Daiwa Europe Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "The market obviously got overextended on the move down to recent lows," he said. "But it doesn't look like we're just going to clear $1.05 straight away. I think the risk is still on the downside." \par \par \uc2  The dollar was consolidative against the yen after Monday's three-yen selloff, gyrating largely around 118 yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was last trading at 118.12/22 against 119.22/27 late in Monday's European trading. \par \par \uc2  An absence of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  allowed yen bulls to push the dollar to a five-week low of 117.68 on Monday, but traders said lingering fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action kept the U.S. unit from reaching the 117.59 June 10 low, where the BOJ's recent rounds of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had begun. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the market would question the resolve of the Japanese authorities to curb what they have called the premature strength of the yen if the BOJ did not step in when the Tokyo market returned from Tuesday's national holiday. ((Shin Kishima, London Capital Markets +44-171-542-6721, shinichi.kishima@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7k045u9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
