\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE- \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  lifts dollar/yen spirits. \b0\par\par\uc2 662 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juli 1999\par 18:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 20 (Reuters) - The dollar pulled away from five-week lows it had set against the yen in the past 24 hours as the U.S. Federal Reserve bought dollars for the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the Bank of Japan on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Fed, which had played no role in a bout of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  orchestrated by the BOJ to stem a soaring yen in June, surprised the markets while Japan was enjoying a public holiday and lifted the dollar about a yen to 119.68 yen. \par \par \uc2  "The market was caught unaware by the Fed," said Bobby Jasper, senior dealer at Fuji Bank in London. "This reinforces their commitment to mantain a managed floating exchange rate." \par \par \uc2  Jasper added he thought the BOJ's preferred trading range for dollar/yen was now between 119 and 123. \par \par \uc2  The Fed action on dollar/yen shifted the market's attention from a prodigious recovery in the euro to above $1.04 which was helped by a stronger-than-expected German Ifo survey. \par \par \uc2  By 1502 GMT the dollar was at 119.10/15 yen, below Tuesday's peak of 119.68 set in the wake of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but around 1.3 percent above Monday's trough of 117.68 yen. \par \par \uc2  BOJ officials confirmed that the Fed had been buying dollars for yen on behalf of the Japanese authorities. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the Fed had used an electronic broking system and added the amounts bought appeared to be small. They also questioned how long dollar/yen would sustain its gains. \par \par \uc2  "There's has been very strong demand for yen and that seems to be coming from international investors who want to get involved in the Japanese stock market," Rob Hayward, senior economist at Bank of America in London, told Reuters TV. \par \par \uc2  "As long as that remains the case then it's likely that the BOJ and perhaps even the Fed on their behalf will continue to have a battle to prevent the yen from getting too strong," Hayward added. \par \par \uc2  Tuesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the first time the BOJ had conducted \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  action via another \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  since June 18, the day it bought euro per yen via the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . On all other occasions since June 10 the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its own account. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that in light of the recent firming trend in the yen, the dollar could have fallen as far as 115 yen and perhaps even 110 in the absence of any \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action. \par \par \uc2  "This latest round of market action at 119 might signal a shift in policy within the BOJ, with 120 as the new target level in dollar/yen," Derek Halpenny, economist at BoT-Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  The sharp dollar/yen rally pushed euro/yen to a high of 124.47 yen from Monday's all-time low at 120.91 yen, while euro/dollar remained unscathed. By session end the euro was at $1.0416/18 from $1.0179/84 late on Tuesday, still benefitting from the positive impact of the June Ifo survey. \par \par \uc2  The report showed Germany's business sentiment index had soared above consensus to 92.9. The survey was seen as heralding an improvement in Europe's economic outlook, which so far has been fairly grim and hurt the euro since its launch. \par \par \uc2  "The German business sentiment survey today solidifies the view that the European economy is doing better," said Hayward. \par \par \uc2  "But there remain a few risk - certainly the domestic side of Germain and Italy might reserve some shocks, particularly on the unemployment side," he added. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that if euro could sustain levels above $1.04 and perhaps move towards $1.05 this could encourage investors to buy the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The euro also soared to a eight-week high of 66.37 pence against sterling, gaining more then a pence from levels seen in late European trading on Monday. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7k0441e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
