\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar down at end of U.S. day after hectic trade. \b0\par\par\uc2 710 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juli 1999\par 23:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, July 19 (Reuters) - The dollar plummeted against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Japanese yen on Monday as traders cited a shift in sentiment about global growth and speculated whether central banks had orchestrated the unexpected drop. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had started the week on firm footing against Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has tumbled with painful regularity since it was launched at the start of the year. \par \par \uc2  But as the dollar lost ground against the yen amid selling by investors looking for Japan's economy to improve, the dollar's sudden weakness filtered through to other markets. \par \par \uc2  At the end of trading there were losses of over 1 percent. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said Tuesday's activity might be similarly hectic, especially as Japanese markets will be closed, reducing fears of possible Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  By the end of trading, dollar/yen was down 1.3 percent at 118.15/25 yen from 119.69/79 yen at the open and only a touch above the day's 117.78 low. \par \par \uc2  Against the euro, the dollar tumbled 1.6 percent leaving the euro at $1.0307/12, safely above the $1.0139/43 open and the euro's life low near $1.0100. Because the gains endured the afternoon, dealers were modestly optimistic that the race to dollar parity has slowed, at least temporarily. \par \par \uc2  In its wake, the dollar fell by 1.7 percent to 1.5575 Swiss francs, and the British pound rose 1.4 percent to $1.5800. \par \par \uc2  "Sentiment is changing a little on Europe and Japan and there was also a lot of technical selling today," said a senior trader at a major U.S. investment bank. \par \par \uc2  Speculation that a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had bought euros enhanced the dollar's sharp decline against the European unit, even though traders later decided \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely. \par \par \uc2  The more likely scenario, traders said, was that the market had been caught short euros, forcing traders to cover some of their positions. \par \par \uc2  New optimism about Japan's economy - reflected by a string of record closes on the benchmark Nikkei 225 stock index and surprisingly strong 1.9 percent growth in the January through March quarter - was increasing the yen's appeal. \par \par \uc2  Traders were also tempted to test the Bank of Japan's resolve to keep a strong yen from choking off \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's nascent economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  It has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tame the yen repeatedly over the past two months and the market had looked for - but did not find - more action as the dollar fell below 120 yen. "The market feels bolder since they weren't around," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  By contrast, the euro's tumble is becoming an embarrassment to the 11 nations that launched it in January, and speculation that European officials may jump in to support the euro were rife. But European leaders have been sending mixed signals and Monday was no exception. \par \par \uc2  "Our traders said the price action looks like it could have been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  even though we have not seen anything," Barclays Capital senior economist Henry Willmore said. \par \par \uc2  Germany's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Bundesbank, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Federal Reserve Bank of New York all declined to comment on the market talk. \par \par \uc2  European officials gave the market mixed signals. The euro's initial weakness had been sparked as German government adviser Herbert Hax predicted the euro's swift fall to $1.00 over the weekend. \par \par \uc2  But comments from European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Vice President Christian Noyer that the euro is now being watched much more closely sparked more speculation about the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Last week European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg had hoisted the euro by hinting European rates may need to rise at some point. This in theory would boost the euro if benign U.S. inflationary data allow the Fed to hold off on another rate hike. \par \par \uc2  In other trading, the dollar also slipped against the Canadian dollar to end at C$1.4897/07 compared with C$1.4913/07 at the open. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar closed at $0.6501/06, extending its recent losses from its $0.6561/66 open. ((-Svea Herbst-Bayliss, NA Treasury Desk, 212-859-1638 )). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7j04a9a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
