\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Yen bulls challenge BOJ resolution. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,255 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juli 1999\par 17:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 19 (Reuters) - Growing scepticism on foreign exchanges about whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb yen strength lifted the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to all-time peaks against the euro and to one-month highs against the dollar on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar began the Asian session around 121 yen but was down near 119.50 yen late in Europe, its lowest since June 18, the day the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on euro/yen via the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The market was expecting the BOJ to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 120.50 yen, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  never came," said Jesper Danneboe, treasury economist at ABN AMRO in London. \par \par \uc2  "Most market players were surprised by the fact the BOJ did not step in and also by the lack of verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese officials throughout the day." \par \par \uc2  The lack of official reaction to a slippage through the key 120 yen level, which many considered a sort of red-alert zone for the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , encouraged dealers to push the dollar as low as 119.58 yen. \par \par \uc2  "I think the market is becoming increasingly sceptical about whether it (the BOJ) can pursue this policy," said Cameron Crise, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Warburg Dillon Read in London. \par \par \uc2  By 1428 GMT the dollar was at 119.56/61 yen from 120.88/02 yen late in Europe on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the euro had slumped as far as 121.01 yen, down 8.5 percent since its launch. It was last at 121.31/35 yen, compared with 123.51/68 yen late on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Steve Barrow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bear Stearns in London, said this was the first session in weeks which had not been dominated by rumours of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The fact that we haven't seen rumours suggests we haven't necessarily seen significant buying coming in, which is not a good sign for dollar/yen," he said. \par \par \uc2  Some wariness about \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action had been initially fuelled by remarks by Eisuke Sakakibara, the former Japanese Finance Ministry's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  czar, about the need to curb the yen stregth in order to allow Japan's economy to bloom. \par \par \uc2  But as the session went by, more and more disbelief about the chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at current levels crept in. \par \par \uc2  A U.S. bank dealer said the market was now focusing on 119 yen, and below that the 117.50/118 area, the level at which the Bank stepped in to curb a yen rally after strong GDP figures. \par \par \uc2  Dealers were also monitoring euro/yen, now at the equivalent of German mark's worst levels against the yen since July 1995. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile euro/yen weakness was preventing the euro from firming against the dollar. Lacking direction in the absence of any key economic data, the euro drifted in the mid $1.01 range for the entire session, trapped in a tight trading band less than half cent away from the $1.0104 life low set last week. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the euro had a chance to regain centre stage on Tuesday with the release of Germany's Ifo economic survey which could shed more light on Europe's economic conditions. \par \par \uc2  Euro/dollar was last trading at $1.0168/73 against an early low of $1.0124 and $1.0214/18 late in Europe on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The euro did not benefit from a drop in 10-year U.S./German government bond yield spread to the lowest level since the launch of the euro on January 4. The spread had plunged on Monday below 93 basis points from peaks near 180 in mid-June. \par \par \uc2  "The narrowing of the spread indicates that the fixed income market is pricing in a stronger growth in Europe over the coming quarters," said Dannesboe. "But this view differs from the view in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market." \par \par \uc2  The euro had suffered earlier from remarks by Germany's "Wise Man" Herbert Hax, who was quoted by the local press at the weekend as saying that the euro could soon fall below $1.00. \par \par \uc2  "Most market players were surprised by the fact the BOJ did not step in and also by the lack of verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese officials throughout the day." \par \par \uc2  The lack of official reaction to a slippage through the key 120 yen level, which many considered a sort of red-alert zone for the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , encouraged dealers to push the dollar as low as 119.58 yen. \par \par \uc2  "I think the market is becoming increasingly sceptical about whether it (the BOJ) can pursue this policy," said Cameron Crise, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Warburg Dillon Read in London. \par \par \uc2  By 1428 GMT the dollar was at 119.56/61 yen from 120.88/02 yen late in Europe on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the euro had slumped as far as 121.01 yen, down 8.5 percent since its launch. It was last at 121.31/35 yen, compared with 123.51/68 yen late on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Steve Barrow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bear Stearns in London, said this was the first session in weeks which had not been dominated by rumours of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The fact that we haven't seen rumours suggests we haven't necessarily seen significant buying coming in, which is not a good sign for dollar/yen," he said. \par \par \uc2  Some wariness about \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action had been initially fuelled by remarks by Eisuke Sakakibara, the former Japanese Finance Ministry's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  czar, about the need to curb the yen stregth in order to allow Japan's economy to bloom. \par \par \uc2  But as the session went by, more and more disbelief about the chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at current levels crept in. \par \par \uc2  A U.S. bank dealer said the market was now focusing on 119 yen, and below that the 117.50/118 area, the level at which the Bank stepped in to curb a yen rally after strong GDP figures. \par \par \uc2  Dealers were also monitoring euro/yen, now at the equivalent of German mark's worst levels against the yen since July 1995. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile euro/yen weakness was preventing the euro from firming against the dollar. Lacking direction in the absence of any key economic data, the euro drifted in the mid $1.01 range for the entire session, trapped in a tight trading band less than half cent away from the $1.0104 life low set last week. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the euro had a chance to regain centre stage on Tuesday with the release of Germany's Ifo economic survey which could shed more light on Europe's economic conditions. \par \par \uc2  Euro/dollar was last trading at $1.0168/73 against an early low of $1.0124 and $1.0214/18 late in Europe on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The euro did not benefit from a drop in 10-year U.S./German government bond yield spread to the lowest level since the launch of the euro on January 4. The spread had plunged on Monday below 93 basis points