\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Euro sets life low, Japan hedgers in quandary. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Masayuki Kitano \par \uc2 872 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juli 1999\par 07:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO July 19 (Reuters) - The euro fell to a record low against the yen on Monday morning and more declines seem likely for the fledgling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid strong selling interest among Japanese investors and exporters, traders and analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The euro fell as low as 121.88 yen, down 9.8 percent from its life high of 135.10 yen set on January 4. \par \par \uc2  Amid weakness in European economic fundamentals, the euro could fall as low as 120 yen in the near term, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The euro stood at 122.01/16 yen as of 0328 GMT, against 123.43 yen in late New York on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the dollar was also lower against the yen, pressured by Japanese exporters, some of which were gradually lowering the level of their offers, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at 120.40/50 yen as of 0333 GMT against 120.95/05 yen in late New York on Friday. The euro's fall on Monday was triggered by comments by German advisory council chief Herbert Hax, who was quoted in Germany's Welt am Sonntag newspaper's Sunday edition as saying the euro could soon fall below parity with the dollar. The report spurred euro selling by overseas operators. Sales by Japanese exporters and investors added to the decline. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said selling by Japanese investors and exporters could accelerate if the market starts to question the ability and resolve of Japanese authorities to rein in the yen's rise. "Up to now, Japanese investors have been enduring the euro's falls, believing that Japanese authorities would push the euro above 125 yen," said Mitsumaru Kumagai, a senior market analyst at the Industrial Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  But if that belief wavers, Kumagai said, Japanese investors could become the main sellers of the euro, replacing overseas players, who have been the main catalyst behind the euro's latest falls by switching out of German bunds and into Japanese stocks. \par \par \uc2  A factor that could boost the yen against both the euro and dollar is growing speculation about disagreement between Japan and the United States on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, which the market feels could force the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) to be less aggressive in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen than before. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top financial diplomat, Haruhiko Kuroda, played down such concerns on Monday by reiterating that cooperation among the Group of Seven industrial nations on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy remained solid. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that unless the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the euro seems set for more falls in the near term. \par \par \uc2  Taisuke Tanaka, global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston, said the need for structural reform in euro zone economies and tight fiscal policies would prevent the euro from rebounding in the short term. \par \par \uc2  "At some point recent rises in European (long-term) interest rates will help boost the euro," but that was not likely to happen soon, Tanaka said. \par \par \uc2  IBJ's Kumagai said waning concerns about a financial crisis in Latin America and the unlikelihood of euro-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  were also factors that made a near-term rebound in the euro improbable. \par \par \uc2  "I think the main scenario is for the ECB to refrain from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost the euro at this point, since there are few signs of inflationary pressure in the euro zone arising from a rise in import prices," Kumagai said. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, jitters that Argentina may default on its debt, a factor that boosted the euro against the dollar last week, have receded, he said. \par \par \uc2  "With the International Monetary Fund ready to offer aid if needed, Argentina seems to have no major funding difficulties in the near term," Kumagai said. \par \par \uc2  Agrentina has to service debt financing of $17 billion in 1999 and is short by $1.9 billion. The IMF has said Argentina is entitled to some $1.3 billion in financial aid if it needs it. In the near term, the market will be focusing on Germany's Ifo business climate survey for June to be released at 0800 GMT on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  In May, the key western German business climate index in the survey rose to 90.4, up from 89.7 in April. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the market expects the index to rise to around 91 in June and a higher figure could spur some euro buying. \par \par \uc2  In any case, analysts said the euro is likely to fare better over the medium term. \par \par \uc2  "The euro may have a bit more room to fall, but it will probably rebound before the end of the year," said Toru Umemoto, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for Morgan Stanley Bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Umemoto said the euro is likely to be supported by a recovery in euro zone economies in the latter half of the year. He also said he could not rule out the possibility of euro-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the ECB as a means of easing upward pressure on euro-zone long-term interest rates. The euro was quoted at $1.0135/45 compared with $1.0199/02 in late New York trade on Friday. ((Masayuki Kitano, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 5473 3748 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \