\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr Drops Vs Yen On Talk Treasury Refused To  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2  By Marianne Sullivan \par \uc2 325 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juli 1999\par 17:19\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar fell early Friday in New York against the Japanese yen on market talk that the U.S. refused a request from Japan to buy dollars for yen. Yet, the U.S. would be unlikely to do so. \par \par \uc2  According to talk, the Treasury turned down a request from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the open market to buy dollars for yen in order to keep the yen from climbing. Treasury officials didn't return phone calls seeking comment. \par \par \uc2  But the scenario isn't likely: While the Treasury can refuse to use its reserves in the open market, the Bank of Japan can tell the Federal Reserve how to use the money it has on deposit with the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Fed - the Treasury's agent for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions - is a custodian of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves for other central banks. Were the Bank of Japan - or any other \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  with reserves on deposit - to ask the Fed to use BOJ reserves for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Fed wouldn't refuse, a spokesman for the New York Federal reserve said. \par \par \uc2  Regardless, the dollar dipped to its lows of the day on the talk, hitting Y120.49. It recently traded at Y120.89, up from Y120.74 early in New York but down slightly from Y120.91 overnight. \par \par \uc2  The dollar is expected to remain in a tight range against the yen, supported by tensions between China and Taiwan. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials have long sought to stem the yen's appreciation, but in the past week some have said a stronger yen is acceptable. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Earlier Friday, for example, Taichi Sakaiya, the director general of\par Japan's Economic Planning Agency, said the dollar at Y120.00 is\par appropriate.\par    -By Marianne Sullivan; 201-938-4385\par email: marianne.sullivan@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 16-07-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1519GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  4214 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv7g05e5i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
