\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CROSS-Euro makes limited capital from ECB scare. \b0\par\par\uc2 614 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juli 1999\par 07:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 16 (Reuters) - The euro was a shade firmer on the yen on Friday after benefiting from scares over European monetary policy and Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The former came when European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg said on Thursday that a tightening bias might gradually creep into the bank's stance if monetary and credit expansion continued. \par \par \uc2  The comment sent euro yields racing higher and lifted the euro as high as 124.03 yen, but on reflection the market decided a hike was implausible given the vulnerable state of the European recovery. \par \par \uc2  "If you read his (Duisenberg's) comments he never used the word 'tightening'," noted one European bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "He said it wasn't an actual bias but a bias gradually creeping in to the bank's thoughts - that's the same as saying don't hold your breath." \par \par \uc2  The sense of disbelief was heightened later by rumours that Duisenberg himself had played down the comments saying the market had overreacted. \par \par \uc2  That saw the euro unwind most of its gains, although by 0500 GMT it was still slightly up at 123.42/60 yen compared to 123.23 in New York and 123.11/16 in Tokyo on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The choppy price action muddied the technical outlook and left analysts divided on its near-term direction. \par \par \uc2  Some thought the failure to make a new low this week pointed to a short-term bottom and looked for a push past 124.00, and ideally 124.44, to confirm the better tone. \par \par \uc2  But even then they emphasised the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have to clear major resistance all the way up to 127.60 to signal a reversal in the six-month-old downtrend. \par \par \uc2  The bears doubted it would even get past 123.60 on Friday and recommended selling for another crack at 122.80 support, which is shielding the lifetime low around 122.55. This camp fully expect the euro to revisit 121.65, the trough from August 1997 derived using the old ecu rate. \par \par \uc2  One wild card, however, is the Bank of Japan, which dealers detected selling yen for dollars late in Tokyo on Thursday, when the dollar had been down at 120.30 yen. \par \par \uc2  Analysts assume the authorities are just as concerned about premature strength in yen/euro as yen/dollar, reflecting in part the potentially ruinous exposure many Japanese institutions have to unhedged euro debt. \par \par \uc2  This was why the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly to support euro/yen last month and why the bank was believed to have buy orders waiting at 122.85 yen, they said. \par \par \uc2  But analysts are unsure how long Japan could hold the line, given it was alone in the use of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Indeed, the authorities' refusal to confirm Thursday's bout of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stoked speculation they were coming under international fire for openly curbing the yen. \par \par \uc2  Market talk is that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors of a number of Japan's Asian neighbours have already expressed their displeasure at the policy, and it was even rumoured the U.S. Federal Reserve had flatly refused a request to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the BOJ's behalf during New York hours. \par \par \uc2  That would be a disturbing development for the BOJ, as all the major central banks have a gentleman's agreement about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on each other's behalf, and it would be very unusual for one to refuse, said analysts. \par \par \uc2  If true, it would mean Japan is becoming increasingly isolated in its attempts to restrain the yen, which in turn should make yen bulls bolder. ((Wayne Cole, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8584 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7g04i9e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
