\par\par \b \uc2  U.S. Treasuries weak despite friendly CPI data. \b0\par\par\uc2 401 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juli 1999\par 07:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 16 (Reuters) - U.S. Treasuries were slightly weaker by Tokyo midday on Thursday, despite market-friendly U.S. price data overnight, as traders grew wary of bullish stock prices and adjusted positions ahead of the weekend. \par \par \uc2  "Despite such good CPI data, underlying sentiment is still weak. I think the market will remain cautious until the next CPI release...and the Dow only seems to go up, backed by good corporate earnings results," said a dealer at a brokerage. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. consumer price index was unchanged in June, easing market worries about inflation, but profit-taking had eroded most gains the end of the New York session on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen's weak tone, despite Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday, also clouded the mood in Tokyo, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  At 0351 GMT, the key 30-year Treasury bond was at 90-21/32 to yield 5.92 percent from 90-22/32, Thursday's New York close. \par \par \uc2  The 5.75 percent two-year note stood at 100-12/32 to yield 5.54 percent, from 100-13/32 in late New York. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think (yen-based investors) will be keen to purchase (Treasuries) given current \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  conditions," the trader said. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, the dollar rose to around 121.30 yen from a four-week low against the yen in late Tokyo trade after the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars for yen. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said a slide in the German bond market overnight also helped erode market sentiment. \par \par \uc2  Euro-zone government bonds fell on Thursday in reaction to a comment by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg that a bias toward tighter interest rates "was gradually creeping into our considerations". \par \par \uc2  Looking ahead, the market will be focusing on Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's Congressional testimony on July 22. \par \par \uc2  "I'll be especially watching what Greenspan will say about U.S. stocks," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The blue-chip Dow Jones Industrial Average finished at 11,186.41 on Thursday, just short of last week's record high close. \par \par \uc2  The September T-bond futures contract stood at 116-24/32, down 0.04 from the previous close. It was moving in a tight range of 116-22/32 to 116-27/32. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 1396 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7g04i8s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
