\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr steady vs yen in Tokyo, focus on China, BOJ. \b0\par\par\uc2 738 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juli 1999\par 09:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO July 16 (Reuters) - The dollar was steady against the yen in late afternoon trade on Friday, supported by safe-haven bids due to tensions between Taiwan and China and the potential for yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ). The dollar was lifted after a Hong Kong newspaper said mainland Chinese military movements had been detected in coastal areas facing Taiwan, dealers said. But its gains were tempered by offers by Japanese exporters around 121.20 yen, they said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at 120.94/99 yen as of 0607 GMT, compared with 120.97/07 yen in late New York trade on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the dollar received a lift in the afternoon after Taiwan stocks fell 6.4 percent amid fears that tensions between Taiwan and China may escalate into military conflict. Safe haven buying was spurred by a report in Hong Kong's Sing Tao Daily which said mainland Chinese military movements had been detected in coastal areas facing Taiwan and some civilian aviation had been disrupted on Thursday to allow military flights. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo dealers said they noticed the Sing Tao report as it was covered by a Japanese newswire service, though Taiwan officials cast serious doubt on the report. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan Defence Ministry spokesman Kung Fan-ting, told Reuters: "I have not heard anything about this." Dealers said impact on the dollar was likely to be limited unless fighting breaks out between China and Taiwan. \par \par \uc2  "The market is now focusing on (Japan's improving) economic prospects and a recent trend of yen-buying will not change so easily," said a dealer for a European bank. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, dealers said they expected the BOJ's Policy Board to keep monetary policy unchanged at Friday's meeting. The board meeting was still in session as of 0632 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The euro stood at $1.0202/05 as of 0635 GMT, compared with $1.0187/94 in late New York on Thursday. Dealers said yen-buying momentum against the dollar had subsided for the time being after the greenback managed to stay above 120.00 yen overnight with the help of dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the BOJ in late Tokyo on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Thursday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the sixth in a recent series that started after Japan's gross domestic product data for the January-March quarter showed unexpectedly robust growth. But unlike in the previous \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Japanese authorities refused to confirm Thursday's action. \par \par \uc2  "I have nothing in particular to say," Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said in a brief news conference on Friday when asked about the apparent yen-selling, dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Separately, Zembei Mizoguchi, head of the Finance Ministry's International Bureau, said on Friday he is watching movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market carefully. \par \par \uc2  Asked about news reports that the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Thursday, Mizuguchi said: "We should not comment whether we did or not." \par \par \uc2  Speculation about an eventual devaluation of the Chinese yuan was another factor helping to blunt rises in the yen. \par \par \uc2  Chinese Premier Zhu Rongji said the government will have to take further steps to boost the slowing economy, the official Financial News reported on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "Zhu's comments seem to indicate that Chinese authorities are desperate to boost the economy," said a dealer at a Japanese city bank. \par \par \uc2  "Speculation of a yuan devaluation could heat up ahead of the 50th anniversary (of the founding of the People's Republic of China) in October," he added. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the euro stood at 123.15/36 yen as of 0650 GMT, against 123.33 yen in late New York. Interest in selling euros for yen was strong at 123.50 yen and above, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The euro gained a slight boost overnight after European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) President Wim Duisenberg hinted at an eventual rise in interest rates for the 11-nation euro zone. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said the euro's gains were short-lived. \par \par \uc2  "Duisenberg's comments were initially positive for the euro because it suggested that euro zone interest rates would not be lowered further," a city bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "But the euro was sold later since Duisenberg's comments triggered a sharp fall in German Bunds" and fuelled concerns of a sell-off of European assets, the dealer said. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk, +81-3-5473-3748 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7g04hq9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
