\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Euro ends up, parity pull remains. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,248 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juli 1999\par 17:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 16 (Reuters) - Traders were left wondering whether the euro was heading higher or still on course for parity as the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended firm against the dollar on Friday, having narrowly avoided a fall to $1.0 earlier in the week. \par \par \uc2  Despite the euro's rise back above $1.02 after sliding to within just over a cent above parity on Tuesday, dealers were wary of pushing it much higher as many bet the rally will run out of steam around $1.03. \par \par \uc2  Parity prophets took comfort in the euro's failure to break through resistance at $1.0280, a level that was still over half a cent away in late European trade. \par \par \uc2  "The risk for a sell-off remains," said Duncan Webster, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HypoVereinsbank in Munich. "The longer we stick around $1.02, the greater the likelihood that the market pushes the euro down to parity. It could get ugly below there." \par \par \uc2  News of a drop to 106.2 in the University of Michigan July consumer sentiment survey from 107.3 pushed the euro to the day's highs of $1.0230. Throughout the session there was little follow-through buying on gains, though subdued trading dominated Friday's session. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.0222/25 at 1441 GMT, compared with $1.0216/21 in late European trading on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  As a gauge for investor expectations, the options market saw no signs that the euro will jump higher in the short-term. Longer term strategies, however, do see a higher euro. \par \par \uc2  "At the moment there is no expectation of a large and quick movement (higher), it is more long-term oriented, strategic position taking," said Andreas Koenig, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  options trader at Commerzbank in London. \par \par \uc2  This was reflected in a modest rise in implied volatility on longer-dated euro/dollar options this week, with one-year vol quoted at 9.2/9.3 percent from 8.8/9.0 on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "Short-term we should go back down. The market wants to see what happens at parity, it is like a magnet," he said. "But longer term we should see $1.08 or $1.10 again." \par \par \uc2  Thursday's comments from European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg, which helped boost the euro by about half a cent, were still a topic for discussion. Duisenberg said the bank might gradually move towards a tightening stance on interest rates if monetary and credit expansion continued. \par \par \uc2  "That added a new piece to the euro puzzle," said Webster. "The risk is that Duisenberg, by starting to talk up interest rates at this stage, has actually damaged the recovery." \par \par \uc2  More official comments on Friday, extolling the longer term virtues of the euro, pulled no punches with the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Verbal support for the euro came from incoming European monetary affairs commissioner Pedro Solbes, who said the success of the euro should not be judged only by its exchange rate, and that bond issuance showed it was a success. \par \par \uc2  Finnish Finance Minister Sauli Niinisto reiterated he saw potential for European growth and for a stronger euro. \par \par \uc2  "It only reminds market participants of the old Keynes' saying 'In the long run we're all dead'," said Webster. "They're not aggressive enough." \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose to overnight highs of 121.12 yen amid concern about tensions between China and Taiwan. Hong Kong's Sing Tao Daily reported mainland Chinese military movements had been detected in coastal areas facing Taiwan. \par \par \uc2  But action on the yen was just as subdued as euro trade as dealers stayed sidelined after talk of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday, when the yen rose towards 120. \par \par \uc2  The market's perception that a yen rise through 120 would not be tolerated by Tokyo was reinforced when Economic Planning Agency chief Taichi Sakaiya said that level was appropriate for Japan's economy. \par \par \uc2  By late Friday, the yen traded at 120.85/95 from late Thursday's 120.50/61. ((Astrid Zweynert, London Capital Markets +44 171 542 6320, with additional reporting by Deepthi Wickremasinghe)). \par \par \uc2  "The risk for a sell-off remains," said Duncan Webster, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HypoVereinsbank in Munich. "The longer we stick around $1.02, the greater the likelihood that the market pushes the euro down to parity. It could get ugly below there." \par \par \uc2  News of a drop to 106.2 in the University of Michigan July consumer sentiment survey from 107.3 pushed the euro to the day's highs of $1.0230. Throughout the session there was little follow-through buying on gains, though subdued trading dominated Friday's session. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.0222/25 at 1441 GMT, compared with $1.0216/21 in late European trading on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  As a gauge for investor expectations, the options market saw no signs that the euro will jump higher in the short-term. Longer term strategies, however, do see a higher euro. \par \par \uc2  "At the moment there is no expectation of a large and quick movement (higher), it is more long-term oriented, strategic position taking," said Andreas Koenig, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  options trader at Commerzbank in London. \par \par \uc2  This was reflected in a modest rise in implied volatility on longer-dated euro/dollar options this week, with one-year vol quoted at 9.2/9.3 percent from 8.8/9.0 on Mon