\par\par \b \uc2  Japan Curr Acct Shrinks As Strong Yen Stifles Exports \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shino Kimura \par \uc2 314 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juli 1999\par 04:28\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's current account surplus contracted for a fourth straight month in May compared to the year before, as the stronger yen reduced the competitiveness of the nation's exports. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Thursday Japan's current account surplus shrank 23.7% to Y1.054 trillion before adjustment for seasonal factors. The surplus was bigger than the consensus forecast of Y892.5 billion by Tokyo economists. \par \par \uc2  The stronger yen, compared to a year ago, rather than dwindling demand abroad, was the main culprit behind the fall in exports, economists said. \par \par \uc2  "It's about exchange rates and not falling overseas interest," said Matthew Poggi, economist at Lehman Brothers Japan Ltd. \par \par \uc2  In May, the dollar averaged around Y122.11, compared with Y135.00 a year ago. Thursday morning the dollar was trading around Y120.60. \par \par \uc2  Recently, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Finance Ministry aggressively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen out of fear that a drop in exports would hurt the economy just as it was trying to recover. But economists say it will be a battle to keep the yen from rising. \par \par \uc2  "Appreciation pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still very strong," Poggi said. \par \par \uc2  Also helping to narrow the surplus in May was a slowing in the pace of decline of imports amid growing domestic demand. \par \par \uc2  Imports fell 2.2% on year, slowing from a 6.7% decline in April. Meanwhile, exports fell by 12.2%, helping to push the May trade surplus down 30.0% on year to Y982.1 billion. \par \par \uc2  While economists were slightly upbeat about the Japanese economy showing signs of a recovery that may support imports in coming months, "the outlook for imports isn't entirely optimistic," said Mamoru Yamazaki, senior economist at Paribas Capital Markets Ltd. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 15-07-99 \par \par \uc2  0228GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv7f05boc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
