\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Dollar eases, euro bounce seen brief. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,314 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juli 1999\par 17:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 15 (Reuters) - The dollar fell against the euro and ended steady versus the yen on Thursday as Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  enjoyed a short-covering rally and the yen was kept in check by talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Two days of euro gains gathered speed after European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg said the bank might gradually move towards a tightening stance on interest rates if monetary policy and credit expansion continued. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said Duisenberg's comments, more hawkish than recent remarks, sparked short-covering on euro/dollar and pushed Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a one-week high of $1.0250. \par \par \uc2  Despite the euro's firmer tone, analysts said it was quetionable whether the market had shrugged off the lure of dollar parity. \par \par \uc2  "The euro got a boost from the comments but it's not going to last," said Cameron Crise, strategist at Warburg Dillion Read. "It's not credible to suggest that the ECB can raise rates with growth still sluggish and inflation so low in Europe." \par \par \uc2  Most recent official data show inflation was 1.0 percent year-on year in May in the 11-member euro zone. Evidence that growth among core countries remains weak came on Thursday when Italy said its industrial output fell 4.6 percent in May. \par \par \uc2  At 1457 GMT the euro was at $1.0240/43, up from $1.0216/21 in late European trading on Wednesday. The dollar was at 120.37/42 yen, compared with 120.48/58, while euro/yen was up at 123.23/42 from 123.08/21. \par \par \uc2  Duisenberg also said the euro had potential to appreciate on as a result of its internal price stability. \par \par \uc2  "The problem for the euro is that while in the longer term it has potential to rise it will still have to face parity because that's how the market is positioned," said a trader at a German bank. "This upmove is going to falter around $1.03 if not before." \par \par \uc2  Euro-zone government bond prices extended losses to a full point on Duisenberg's comments. The benchmark 10-year cash Bund price was down nearly a point to yield 4.78 percent. Euro short-term interest rate futures implied rates from September 2000 onwards between 20 and 23 basis points higher. The ECB kept its refinancing rate steady at 2.5 percent on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the dollar steadied in the mid-120-yen area after a rollercoaster ride that sparked a one-yen rise from a low near 120.30 amid talk in the Tokyo market that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the yen's rise for the sixth time in little over a month. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  talk came amid concern that a stronger yen will put Japan nascent recovery at risk by choking off exporters' profits. \par \par \uc2  But as the followthrough was only limited and the BOJ declined to comment on the action, some dealers ruled ot \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar buying and said the BOJ had merely been checking dealing rates and that part of the move was options related. \par \par \uc2  Options dealers said an Asian institution was seen buying dollar/yen around the time the BOJ was checking rates, possibly to protect a knockout option which would become worthless if the dollar fell below 120 yen. \par \par \uc2  The constant threat of BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely to keep the dollar supported at 120 yen, analysts said. "That does seem to be the line in the sand," Rob Hayward, economic adviser at Bank of America, told Reuters Television. \par \par \uc2  Top Japanese financial diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda declined to comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Zembei Mizoguchi, head of the Finance Ministry's International Bureau, said the authorities were closely watching the market. \par \par \uc2  "The market is likely to remain on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alert around those levels," said Michael Wallace, manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysis at Standard and Poor's MMS. "And (Japanese) officials seem to stepping up their verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  The dollar remained steady as U.S. inflation data showed the consumer price index was unchanged in June after also being flat in May. The core rate, which strips out volatile food and energy components, expanded 0.1 percent, slightly less than expected. \par \par \uc2  Despite the euro's firmer tone, analysts said it was quetionable whether the market had shrugged off the lure of dollar parity. \par \par \uc2  "The euro got a boost from the comments but it's not going to last," said Cameron Crise, strategist at Warburg Dillion Read. "It's not credible to suggest that the ECB can raise rates with growth still sluggish and inflation so low in Europe." \par \par \uc2  Most recent official data show inflation was 1.0 percent year-on year in May in the 11-member euro zone. Evidence that growth among core countries remains weak came on Thursday when Italy said its industrial output fell 4.6 percent in May. \par \par \uc2  At 1457 GMT the euro was at $1.0240/43, up from $1.0216/21 in late European trading on Wednesday. The dollar was at 120.37/42 yen, compared with 120.48/58, while euro/yen was up at 123.23/42 from 123.08/21. \par \par \uc2  Duisenberg also said the euro had potential to appreciate on as a result of its internal price stability. \par \par \uc2  "The problem for the euro is that while in the longer term it has potential to rise it will still have to face parity because that's how the market is positioned," said a trader at a German bank. "This upmove is going to falter around $1.03 if not before." \par \par \uc2  Euro-zone government bond prices extended losses to a full point on Duisenberg's comments. The benchmark 10-year cash Bund price was down nearly a point to yield 4.78 percent. Euro short-term interest rate futures implied rates from September 2000 onwards between 20 and 23 basis points higher. The ECB kept its refinancing rate steady at 2.5 percent on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the dollar steadied in the mid-120-yen area after a rollercoaster ride that sparked a one-yen rise from a low near 120.30 amid talk in the Tokyo market that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the yen's rise for the sixth time in little over a month. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  talk came amid concern that a stronger yen will put Japan's nascent recovery at risk by choking off exporters' profits. \par \par \uc2  But as the followthrough was only limited and the BOJ declined to comment on the action, some dealers ruled out \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar buying and said the BOJ had merely been checking rates and that part of the move was options related. \par \par \uc2  Options dealers said an Asian institution was seen buying dollar/yen around the time the BOJ was checking rates, possibly to protect a knockout option which would become worthless if the dollar fell below 120 yen. \par \par \uc2  The constant threat of BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely to keep the dollar supported at 120 yen, analysts said. "That does seem to be the line in the sand," Rob Hayward, economic adviser at Bank of 